TP.register({id:"lp4-g8",lp:4,grade:"8",mode:"baseline",nextGrade:"high school",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY=`<p>The cold came in hard on Thursday, and by Friday morning the pond behind the village hall lay under a lid of gray ice. Snow had fallen all night. Fresh snow settles over everything in one smooth layer, and the whole valley looked like a loaf of bread before it is sliced.</p><p>Nadia walked the edge of the pond before school, the way her grandmother had taught her. Near the inlet, where the creek slides in under the bank, the ice was darker. She tapped it with the toe of her boot and heard a sound like a spoon on an empty bowl.</p><p>Her brother Teo was already at the rink side, lacing up. “Nine days,” he said. “The relay is in nine days.” Teo had waited for this ice the way a farmer waits for rain. He had skated the village relay every winter since he was six, and this was the first year he was fast enough to anchor a team.</p><p>“The inlet is thin,” Nadia said.</p><p>“It is always thin at the inlet.”</p><p>“Not like this.”</p><p>Teo stopped pulling his laces. He looked at the gray patch, then at the orange cones that marked the relay lanes, then at his skates. “Somebody has to tell Mr. Alvarez before Saturday,” he said. “I would say it myself. I would just sound like a kid who is scared of the race.”</p><p>Out on the path their father was shoveling the walk to the warming hut for the second time that morning. Snow kept filling it in behind him. He called it his Sisyphus hill and laughed, scraping.</p><p>Mr. Alvarez kept the rink, and on cold mornings he kept the kettle on in the warming hut. Nadia stood on the bank until her fingers went stiff inside her mittens. The hut window was gold with lamplight. When she finally knocked, the sound went flat into the cold, as if the air itself had swallowed it.</p>`;const TEXT_A=`<p class="src">Text A: “Close Cobbler’s Hill Road to Cars” by Dr. Amara Whitfield, nurse practitioner at the Birchfold Valley Clinic. Letter to the editor, <em>Birchfold Weekly Ledger</em>, January 9, 2026.</p><p>Birchfold should close Cobbler’s Hill Road to cars from December 1 to March 1. Plows and ambulances would still use it, and one gate at the bottom would keep everything else off. The village spends $26,000 a year keeping that single road salted and bare. Closing it to cars would save about $18,000 of that.</p><p>Safety is the usual worry, and safety is exactly my point. Cobbler’s Hill is the only sledding hill in the village, and children sled on it whether the road is open or not. The town of Kettle Falls closed its hill road in 2023. Its fire department logged 14 emergency calls for sled crashes the winter before the change and 9 the winter after.</p><p>Quiet roads help animals in winter too. Biologists at the state college found that deer move through this valley at dusk and are struck most often on steep, salted roads. My own niece sledded on Cobbler’s Hill every Saturday last winter and never once had to pull her sled out of the way of a car. And let us remember that the public works director who wants the road open is the same man who lost the plow key for two days last January.</p><p>We can spend less, keep children out of traffic, and give the village a free hill.</p>`;const TEXT_B=`<p class="src">Text B: “Keep the Hill Road Open” by Hugo Prazak, owner of Summit Ski and Sled Shop and chair of the Birchfold Merchants Group. Guest column, <em>Birchfold Weekly Ledger</em>, January 16, 2026.</p><p>Dr. Whitfield says closing the road makes children safer. Kettle Falls says otherwise. The clinic there treated 6 broken wrists from sledding the winter before the road closed and 11 the winter after. More sledders on a hill with nobody watching is not safety.</p><p>Her savings number is wrong too. The public works office puts the savings at $11,000 a year, not $18,000, and the gate and its wiring would cost $9,000.</p><p>Eleven families live above the gate. In February they would carry groceries up a quarter mile of packed ice. My shop sits at the top of that road, and so does the village sled rental. A village that locks its only hill road is telling its older neighbors to stay home.</p><p>If we want a safe hill, mark a sledding lane, post a crossing sign, and plow the road the way we always have.</p>`;const TEXTS=()=>`<div class="texts"><div class="passage small-p">${TEXT_A}</div><div class="passage small-p">${TEXT_B}</div></div>`;function gridFig(o){const m={l:o.yl?64:34,r:20,t:18,b:o.xl?54:30},sx=o.sx||30,sy=o.sy||30,W=(o.x1-o.x0)*sx,H=(o.y1-o.y0)*sy;const X=x=>m.l+(x-o.x0)*sx,Y=y=>m.t+(o.y1-y)*sy,gx=o.gx||1,gy=o.gy||1,lx=o.lx||gx,ly=o.ly||gy;const ax=X(Math.max(o.x0,0)),ay=Y(Math.max(o.y0,0)),quad=o.x0<0||o.y0<0;let s=`<svg class="${o.cls||""}" viewBox="0 0 ${W+m.l+m.r} ${H+m.t+m.b}" width="${o.w||W+m.l+m.r}" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--surface)"/>`;for(let x=o.x0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=gx)s+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;for(let y=o.y0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=gy)s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${ay}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${ay}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="${ax}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${ax}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;const fs=o.fs||13;for(let x=Math.ceil(o.x0/lx)*lx;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=lx){if(quad&&x===0)continue;s+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${ay+fs+3}" font-size="${fs}" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${x}</text>`}for(let y=Math.ceil(o.y0/ly)*ly;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=ly){if(quad&&y===0)continue;s+=`<text x="${ax-6}" y="${Y(y)}" font-size="${fs}" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${y}</text>`}if(o.xl)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="${m.t+H+44}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;if(o.yl)s+=`<text transform="translate(16,${m.t+H/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.yl}</text>`;(o.polys||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polygon points="${p.pts.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="${p.fill||"var(--gold)"}" fill-opacity=".55" stroke="${p.c||"var(--ink)"}" stroke-width="2.5"/>`});(o.paths||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polyline points="${p.pts.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="${p.c||"var(--sea)"}" stroke-width="3.5" stroke-linejoin="round" ${p.dash?'stroke-dasharray="9 6"':""}/>`});(o.pts||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="${o.pr||5.5}" fill="${p[3]||"var(--coral)"}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2"/>`});(o.labels||[]).forEach(l=>{s+=`<text x="${X(l[0])+(l[3]||0)}" y="${Y(l[1])+(l[4]||0)}" font-size="${l[5]||16}" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="${l[6]||"start"}" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="4">${l[2]}</text>`});return s+"</svg>"}function snowGraph(){return gridFig({alt:"Graph of centimeters of snow on the ground against hours since the storm started for Station A, a straight line through the origin",x0:0,x1:6,y0:0,y1:24,sx:64,sy:12,gy:4,xl:"Hours since the storm started",yl:"Snow on the ground (cm)",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[6,24]]}],pts:[[2,8],[4,16]],labels:[[.6,20,"Station A"]]})}function planGraph(){return gridFig({alt:"Graph of total cost in hundreds of dollars against number of storms plowed for Plan A and Plan B. The two lines cross.",x0:0,x1:8,y0:0,y1:32,sx:48,sy:9,gy:4,ly:8,xl:"Storms plowed in the winter",yl:"Total cost (hundreds of dollars)",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[8,32]]},{pts:[[0,10],[8,26]],c:"var(--coral)",dash:true}],labels:[[2.3,5.5,"Plan A",0,0,16,"start"],[.5,15.5,"Plan B",0,0,16,"start"]]})}function curve(f,a,b,flip){const p=[];for(let t=a;t<=b+1e-9;t+=.1)p.push(flip?[f(t),t]:[t,f(t)]);return p}function ring(r){const p=[];for(let t=0;t<=6.2833;t+=.08)p.push([r*Math.cos(t),r*Math.sin(t)]);p.push([r,0]);return p}function funcGraphs(){const base={x0:-4,x1:4,y0:-4,y1:4,sx:34,sy:34,fs:13};const P=gridFig(Object.assign({alt:"Graph P, a V shape with its point at 0, negative 2",paths:[{pts:curve(x=>Math.abs(x)-2,-3.5,3.5)}]},base));const Q=gridFig(Object.assign({alt:"Graph Q, a circle of radius 3 centered at the origin",paths:[{pts:ring(3)}]},base));return`<div class="figure"><div>${P}<p class="fig-label">Graph P</p></div><div>${Q}<p class="fig-label">Graph Q</p></div></div>`}function funcTables(){const t=(n,xs,ys)=>`<div><table class="chart"><tr><th>Input</th>${xs.map(x=>`<td>${x}</td>`).join("")}</tr><tr><th>Output</th>${ys.map(y=>`<td>${y}</td>`).join("")}</tr></table><p class="fig-label" style="font-size:1.1rem;margin:4px 0 0">Table ${n}</p></div>`;return`<div class="figure">${t("A",[1,2,3,4],[6,10,14,18])}${t("B",[3,5,5,8],[2,4,9,11])}</div>`}function sledGrid(){return gridFig({alt:"Coordinate plane with triangle DEF. D is at 1, 2. E is at 5, 1. F is at 3, 5.",x0:-7,x1:11,y0:-6,y1:11,sx:24,sy:24,fs:11,lx:2,ly:2,polys:[{pts:[[1,2],[5,1],[3,5]]}],pts:[[1,2],[5,1],[3,5]],labels:[[1,2,"D",-18,6],[5,1,"E",8,8],[3,5,"F",-4,-10]]})}function triFigs(){const tri=`<svg viewBox="-40 0 310 200" width="310" role="img" aria-label="Right triangle with legs 20 meters and 15 meters and hypotenuse c"><polygon points="30,160 230,160 30,10" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="30" y="144" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="130" y="186" text-anchor="middle" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">20 m</text><text x="22" y="88" text-anchor="end" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">15 m</text><text x="146" y="70" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">c</text></svg>`;const wire=`<svg viewBox="0 0 250 235" width="250" role="img" aria-label="A trail sign post with a 17 foot wire running from the ground to a point h feet up the post. The wire is anchored 8 feet from the base of the post."><line x1="10" y1="205" x2="240" y2="205" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="172" y="20" width="12" height="185" fill="var(--muted)"/><rect x="140" y="22" width="76" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="178" y="41" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">TRAIL</text><line x1="76" y1="205" x2="174" y2="60" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/><text x="96" y="128" text-anchor="end" font-size="19" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">17 ft</text><text x="124" y="226" text-anchor="middle" font-size="19" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">8 ft</text><text x="196" y="140" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">h</text><rect x="158" y="191" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/></svg>`;const box=`<svg viewBox="0 0 350 200" width="350" role="img" aria-label="A crate 12 inches long, 8 inches wide, and 9 inches tall, with a dashed line from one bottom corner to the opposite top corner"><g fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"><polygon points="20,70 220,70 220,160 20,160"/><polygon points="20,70 70,30 270,30 220,70"/><polygon points="220,70 270,30 270,120 220,160"/></g><line x1="20" y1="160" x2="270" y2="30" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="3.5" stroke-dasharray="8 6"/><text x="120" y="182" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">12 in. long</text><text x="252" y="152" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">8 in.</text><text x="278" y="76" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">9 in.</text></svg>`;return`<div class="figure"><div>${tri}<p class="fig-label" style="font-size:1.1rem">Sled run triangle</p></div><div>${wire}<p class="fig-label" style="font-size:1.1rem">Trail sign wire</p></div><div>${box}<p class="fig-label" style="font-size:1.1rem">Rental crate</p></div></div>`}const SC=[[6,8],[8,11],[10,14],[12,15],[16,20],[18,24],[20,26],[22,31],[26,33],[28,38],[30,41],[24,9]];function scatter(){return gridFig({alt:"Scatter plot of sleds rented against centimeters of snow on the ground. Points rise from lower left to upper right, with one point far below the others.",x0:0,x1:32,y0:0,y1:45,sx:14,sy:7,gx:2,gy:5,lx:4,ly:10,xl:"Snow on the ground (cm)",yl:"Sleds rented that day",pts:SC})}const WT_ORDER=["informative","narrative","argument"];const SAY={argument:{prompt:"Should Birchfold close Cobbler’s Hill Road to cars from December 1 to March 1? Take 1 minute to plan. Then present your claim, your strongest reasons, and evidence from Text A and Text B. Say what the other side would argue and why you still disagree. Talk for 1 to 2 minutes, look at the camera, and speak clearly.",pills:["My claim","Reason and evidence","The other side","So..."],key:["States a clear claim and stays focused on it (SL.8.4)","Emphasizes the most important points in a logical order (SL.8.4)","Backs the claim with relevant evidence from the texts and sound reasoning (SL.8.4)","Uses well-chosen details, including what the other side says (SL.8.4)","Eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, clear pronunciation (SL.8.4)"]},informative:{prompt:"Take 1 minute to plan. Then explain the Cobbler’s Hill Road debate to someone who has not read the texts. Tell what each side wants and the strongest evidence each side gives. Talk for 1 to 2 minutes, look at the camera, and speak clearly.",pills:["The question","One side","The other side","Wrap up"],key:["Introduces the topic and stays focused on it (SL.8.4)","Emphasizes the most important points in a logical order (SL.8.4)","Uses relevant evidence from both texts, such as the $18,000 and $11,000 savings numbers or the Kettle Falls counts (SL.8.4)","Uses well-chosen details and explains them accurately (SL.8.4)","Eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, clear pronunciation (SL.8.4)"]},narrative:{prompt:"Take 1 minute to plan. Then tell a story, real or made up, about a time the weather changed someone’s plans. Set the scene, tell what happened in order, and end by telling what the person learned. Talk for 1 to 2 minutes, look at the camera, and speak clearly.",pills:["Set the scene","The change","What happened","What it meant"],key:["Establishes a context and a point of view (SL.8.4)","Presents events in a logical sequence with transitions (SL.8.4)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing, description, or sensory language (SL.8.4)","Ends with a conclusion that reflects on the experience (SL.8.4)","Eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, clear pronunciation (SL.8.4)"]}};const pills=a=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:20px;gap:14px">${a.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.1rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const lookBack=open=>`<details class="look-back no-key"${open?" open":""}><summary>Look back at Text A and Text B</summary>${TEXTS()}</details>`;const WT_BY_STD={"W.8.1":"argument","W.9-10.1":"argument","W.8.2":"informative","W.8.3":"narrative"};function curWT(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:st?[st]:[];const p=ps.find(x=>x&&x.wt);if(p)return p.wt;for(const q of ps)for(const c of q&&q.std||[])if(WT_BY_STD[c])return WT_BY_STD[c];for(const c of st&&st.std||[])if(WT_BY_STD[c])return WT_BY_STD[c];return"argument"}function isTexts(st){return st.id===2||st.id===3||st.id==="L2"}function writeStats(t){const w=(t.trim().match(/\S+/g)||[]).length,s=(t.match(/[^.!?]+[.!?]+/g)||[]).length;return`${w} words, about ${s} sentences`}const METHODS=["Typed, spellcheck on","Typed, spellcheck off","Voice typing / dictation","Handwritten, shown on camera or photo","Dictated to teacher or parent (scribed)"];function wbox(id,label,what){const get=k=>(E.state[k]||{})[id]||"";const set=(k,v)=>{const S=E.state;S[k]=S[k]||{};S[k][id]=v;E.save()};return{render:()=>{const w=get("written"),m=get("method");return`<label class="small" style="display:block;margin-top:16px">${label}<textarea class="wbox" data-wb="1">${E.esc(w)}</textarea></label><p class="small wstats" data-ws="1">${w.trim()?writeStats(w):""}</p><label class="small" style="display:block;margin:6px 0 0">Teacher: how the student produced the writing <select data-wm="1" style="font:inherit;padding:4px 8px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink)"><option value="">Choose</option>${METHODS.map(x=>`<option${m===x?" selected":""}>${x}</option>`).join("")}</select></label>`},bind:root=>{const ta=root.querySelector("[data-wb]"),ws=root.querySelector("[data-ws]"),ms=root.querySelector("[data-wm]");if(ta)ta.oninput=()=>{set("written",ta.value);if(ws)ws.textContent=ta.value.trim()?writeStats(ta.value):""};if(ms)ms.onchange=()=>set("method",ms.value)},printHtml:()=>{const w=get("written").trim(),m=get("method"),line="__________________________________________________________";return`<p><strong>How the writing was produced:</strong> ${m?E.esc(m):"&#9744; typed &nbsp; &#9744; voice typing &nbsp; &#9744; handwritten &nbsp; &#9744; adult scribed"}</p><p><strong>${what}${w?" ("+writeStats(w)+")":""}:</strong><br>${w?E.esc(w).replace(/\n/g,"<br>"):"<br>"+line+"<br><br>"+line+"<br><br>"+line}</p>`}}}function wlead(wt){const same=E.state.say===wt;if(wt==="argument")return same?"Write the argument you just presented.":"Should Birchfold close Cobbler’s Hill Road to cars from December 1 to March 1? Write an argument.";return same?"Write the story you just told.":"Write a story about a time the weather changed someone’s plans."}const WT_ALT={argument:{label:"Argument",icon:"⚖️"},informative:{label:"Informative",icon:"📰"},narrative:{label:"Narrative",icon:"📖"}};const SAY_HINT={argument:"Take a side on the hill road",informative:"Explain both sides of the debate",narrative:"Tell a story about a change of plans"};const WRITE_HINT={argument:"Claim, reasons, and evidence",informative:"Explain the hill road debate",narrative:"A story about a change of plans"};const altFor=(group,wt,hints)=>Object.assign({group,key:wt,hint:hints[wt]},WT_ALT[wt]);const WBOX_NOTES=wbox(3,"Student’s research notes (type or paste here)","Student’s research notes");const WBOX_WRITE=wbox(6,"Student’s writing (type or paste here)","Student writing");const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Thin Ice Story",short:"Story",parts:[{title:"Read and Cite",std:["RL.8.1"],dur:135,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove your answer.",html:()=>`<div class="passage sm">${STORY}</div>`,qs:["How badly does Teo want to skate in the relay? Read me the one sentence that most strongly supports your answer.","The story never says what Nadia tells Mr. Alvarez. What do you think she decided to do? Read me the words that most strongly support your inference."],key:["He wants it badly. Strongest evidence: “Teo had waited for this ice the way a farmer waits for rain.” Weaker choices: “Nine days,” or that he had skated every winter since he was six, or that he was finally fast enough to anchor a team. Full credit for the farmer sentence. (RL.8.1, strongest evidence)","She decided to report the thin ice at the inlet. Strongest evidence: “When she finally knocked” at the warming hut window, where Mr. Alvarez keeps the kettle on. May add Teo’s line that somebody has to tell Mr. Alvarez, and that she stood on the bank until her fingers went stiff. (RL.8.1, inference)"]},{title:"Dialogue and Events",std:["RL.8.3"],dur:105,dir:"Answer out loud. Read me the line you are talking about.",qs:["Which line of dialogue pushes Nadia toward her decision at the end? Explain how that line does it.","Teo says, “I would just sound like a kid who is scared of the race.” What does that line reveal about him?"],key:["“Somebody has to tell Mr. Alvarez before Saturday.” Explains that Teo names the job that has to be done but rules himself out, which leaves it to Nadia and leads to her knocking on the hut window. Also accept the full line with “I would say it myself.” (RL.8.3, dialogue provokes a decision)","He is torn and honest. He wants the relay badly, he believes his sister about the ice, and he is thinking about how other people would read him if he spoke up. (RL.8.3, dialogue reveals character)"]},{title:"Word Choice",std:["RL.8.4"],dur:120,dir:"Answer out loud.",qs:["The story says the valley “looked like a loaf of bread before it is sliced.” What does that comparison mean, and what feeling does it create?","At the end, Nadia’s knock “went flat into the cold, as if the air itself had swallowed it.” How does that word choice affect the tone of the ending?","Her father calls the walk he keeps shoveling his “Sisyphus hill.” In a Greek myth, Sisyphus had to push a boulder up a hill forever, and every time he neared the top it rolled back down. What is her father suggesting about shoveling?"],key:["The new snow covered the whole valley in one smooth, unbroken layer, with no tracks or edges showing. Creates a calm, quiet, untouched feeling. (RL.8.4, figurative meaning)","Lonely, uncertain, tense. The sound gets no answer, so her brave act feels small and the ending feels unfinished rather than triumphant. (RL.8.4, word choice and tone)","Shoveling never ends, because the snow keeps falling and fills the walk in again as fast as he clears it. He is joking about work that repeats forever. (RL.8.4, allusion)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Hill Road Debate",short:"2 Texts",parts:[{title:"Cite the Texts",std:["RI.8.1"],dur:110,dir:"Read both texts to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove your answer.",html:()=>TEXTS(),qs:["Dr. Whitfield claims closing the road is safer for children. Which evidence in Text A most strongly supports that claim? Read it to me.","Who does Mr. Prazak believe would be hurt most by closing the road? Read me the words that best support your inference."],key:["Kettle Falls closed its hill road in 2023, and its fire department “logged 14 emergency calls for sled crashes the winter before the change and 9 the winter after.” (RI.8.1, strongest evidence)","The eleven families who live above the gate, especially older neighbors. Cites “In February they would carry groceries up a quarter mile of packed ice” or “telling its older neighbors to stay home.” (RI.8.1, inference)"]},{title:"Judge the Argument",std:["RI.8.8"],dur:150,dir:"Answer out loud.",qs:["What is Dr. Whitfield’s claim in Text A? What reasons does she give for it?","Find one piece of evidence in Text A that has nothing to do with her claim, and one that is too weak to prove much. Explain each choice.","Mr. Prazak says closing the road made sledding less safe in Kettle Falls because broken wrists went from 6 to 11. Is that sound reasoning? What else would you need to know?"],key:["Claim: close Cobbler’s Hill Road to cars from December 1 to March 1. Reasons: it saves money, it keeps children sledding out of traffic, and quiet roads help animals in winter. Needs the claim and at least two reasons. (RI.8.8, delineate the argument)","Irrelevant: the public works director losing the plow key for two days. That attacks the man and says nothing about whether the road should close. Too weak: her niece sledding every Saturday without dodging a car. That is one child and one winter. (RI.8.8, irrelevant and insufficient evidence)","Not sound. The numbers are small and cover one winter, and if more children sledded there after the road closed, the count of hurt wrists could rise while the risk for each sledder falls. Would want to know: how many children sledded each winter, whether those injuries happened on that hill, what other years show, and what happened to crashes with cars. Accept any answer that questions cause and asks for more evidence. (RI.8.8, soundness of reasoning)"]},{title:"Where They Disagree",std:["RI.8.9"],dur:95,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from both texts.",qs:["Find a place where the two texts disagree about a fact. Give the detail from each text.","Both writers use Kettle Falls as evidence, but they reach opposite conclusions. How is that possible? Is this a disagreement about facts or about how to interpret them?"],key:["The yearly savings. Text A says about $18,000. Text B says the public works office puts it at $11,000. (RI.8.9, disagreement of fact)","They count different things. Whitfield counts fire department emergency calls for sled crashes (14 then 9). Prazak counts broken wrists treated at the clinic (6 then 11). Both counts could be true, so it is a disagreement about interpretation, meaning which measure shows whether children got safer. (RI.8.9, disagreement of interpretation)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Snow Report Notes",short:"Research",parts:[{std:["W.8.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:135,dir:"Type your answers in the notes box.",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:18px">Research notes: Should Birchfold close Cobbler’s Hill Road to cars in winter?</p>${lookBack(true)}<ol class="qlist"><li>Paraphrase one piece of evidence from each text. Use your own words, not the author’s. Write A or B after each one.</li><li>Add one exact quote from either text. Put it in quotation marks and name the author.</li><li>Which author would you trust more about injuries? Which author might gain or lose money depending on the vote? Write one sentence about how that affects how much you trust each one.</li><li>List both sources with the author, title, newspaper, and date. Then write the search terms you would type to check whether sledding injuries really changed in Kettle Falls.</li></ol>`,...WBOX_NOTES,key:["Paraphrases one piece of evidence from each text accurately, not copied word for word, and marks the source (W.8.8)","One exact quote in quotation marks with the author named (W.8.8)","Judges credibility: Whitfield is a clinic nurse practitioner citing a fire department log, so stronger on injuries than on the budget; Prazak owns the shop at the top of the road and the sled rental is up there too, so he has a money stake, though he knows that road firsthand (W.8.8)","Source information for both texts (author, title, newspaper, date) and specific search terms, such as: Kettle Falls hill road closed 2023 sledding injuries fire department report (W.8.8)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Verb Flurry",short:"Verbs",parts:[{std:["L.8.1"],dur:115,dir:"Answer out loud. Say each fixed sentence the correct way.",qs:["“Shoveling the warming hut steps took Nadia twenty minutes.” Which word is the verbal? What kind is it, and what job does it do in the sentence?","“Teo hoped to anchor the relay team.” Find the verbal and name its kind.","“The drifting snow hid the trail markers.” Find the verbal and name its kind. What does it describe?","Change this sentence to active voice: “The village sidewalks were cleared by the plow crew before dawn.”","Fix the verb: “If I was the rink keeper, I would test the ice every morning.”","Fix the shift in this sentence: “Nadia laced her skates, and then the ice was tested by her.”"],key:["Shoveling. Gerund. It is the subject of the sentence (acts as a noun). (L.8.1, verbals)","to anchor. Infinitive. (L.8.1, verbals)","drifting. Participle. It describes snow (acts as an adjective). (L.8.1, verbals)","The plow crew cleared the village sidewalks before dawn. (L.8.1, active and passive voice)","If I were the rink keeper, I would test the ice every morning. (L.8.1, subjunctive mood)","Nadia laced her skates and then tested the ice. Both verbs are now active. (L.8.1, shift in voice)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Warming Hut Talk",short:"Speak",parts:WT_ORDER.map(wt=>({wt,alt:altFor("say",wt,SAY_HINT),std:["SL.8.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:135,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes.",html:()=>`<p class="big">${SAY[wt].prompt}</p>${pills(SAY[wt].pills)}${wt==="narrative"?"":lookBack()}`,after:()=>{E.state.say=wt;E.save()},key:SAY[wt].key}))},{id:6,title:"Write by the Fire",short:"Write",parts:[{wt:"argument",alt:altFor("write","argument",WRITE_HINT),std:["W.8.1","L.8.1"],n:8,label:"checks met",dur:270,dir:"Type in the box, or write on paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<p class="big">${wlead("argument")} Write one strong paragraph of 8 to 10 sentences.</p><ul class="qlist"><li>State your claim</li><li>Name the other side’s claim and show how yours is different</li><li>Give reasons with evidence from Text A or Text B, and name the text</li><li>Use linking words like because, however, for example, as a result</li><li>End with a conclusion</li></ul>${lookBack()}`,...WBOX_WRITE,key:["Introduces the claim clearly (W.8.1)","Acknowledges the opposing claim and distinguishes it from their own (W.8.1)","Organizes reasons and evidence logically (W.8.1)","Supports the claim with relevant evidence from the texts and names the source (W.8.1)","Uses words, phrases, or clauses that connect claim, counterclaim, reasons, and evidence (W.8.1)","Keeps a formal style (W.8.1)","Concluding statement follows from and supports the argument (W.8.1)","Verbs are used correctly: voice and mood stay consistent (L.8.1)"]},{wt:"informative",alt:altFor("write","informative",WRITE_HINT),std:["W.8.2","L.8.1"],n:8,label:"checks met",dur:270,dir:"Type in the box, or write on paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to explain the Cobbler’s Hill Road debate. Write 8 to 10 sentences. Give your writing a heading at the top.</p><ul class="qlist"><li>Introduce the topic and tell what you will cover</li><li>Organize it by side: what each side wants and why</li><li>Use facts, details, or a quote from each text</li><li>Use topic words like gate, estimate, evidence, emergency calls</li><li>End with a conclusion</li></ul>${lookBack()}`,...WBOX_WRITE,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and previews what follows, with a heading (W.8.2)","Organizes information into broader categories, such as each side of the debate (W.8.2)","Develops the topic with relevant, well-chosen facts, details, or quotations (W.8.2)","Uses varied transitions to connect ideas (W.8.2)","Uses precise language and topic vocabulary (W.8.2)","Keeps a formal style (W.8.2)","Concluding statement follows from the explanation (W.8.2)","Verbs are used correctly: voice and mood stay consistent (L.8.1)"]},{wt:"narrative",alt:altFor("write","narrative",WRITE_HINT),std:["W.8.3","L.8.1"],n:7,label:"checks met",dur:270,dir:"Type in the box, or write on paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<p class="big">${wlead("narrative")} Write 10 to 12 sentences. It can be real or made up.</p>${pills(["Set the scene","The change","What happened","What it meant"])}<ul class="qlist"><li>Include at least two lines of dialogue</li><li>Use details the reader can see, hear, or feel</li><li>End by reflecting on what happened</li></ul>`,...WBOX_WRITE,key:["Establishes a context and point of view and introduces a narrator or characters (W.8.3)","Events unfold naturally and logically (W.8.3)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing, description, or reflection (W.8.3)","Uses transitions to show sequence and shifts in time or setting (W.8.3)","Uses precise words, descriptive details, and sensory language (W.8.3)","Conclusion follows from and reflects on the events (W.8.3)","Verbs are used correctly: voice and mood stay consistent (L.8.1)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Ice and Roots",short:"Numbers",parts:[{title:"Rational or Not",std:["8.NS.A.1"],dur:115,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up or tell your teacher each answer.",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="text-align:center;letter-spacing:.02em">0.6 &nbsp;&nbsp; √10 &nbsp;&nbsp; 0.818181... &nbsp;&nbsp; −<sup>7</sup>⁄<sub>8</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; 2.030030003... &nbsp;&nbsp; <sup>11</sup>⁄<sub>3</sub></p>`,qs:["Sort the six numbers above into two groups: rational and irrational. How did you decide?","Write <sup>5</sup>⁄<sub>6</sub> as a decimal. What do you notice about the digits?","Write 0.555... (the 5 repeats forever) as a fraction. Show your steps."],key:["Rational: 0.6, 0.818181..., −7/8, 11/3. Irrational: √10, 2.030030003... Explains that rational numbers can be written as a fraction of two integers and their decimals end or repeat; irrational decimals go on forever with no repeating block. Partial: the six sorted right with no account of how is approaching, and so is one misplaced number with the reasoning sound. (8.NS.A.1)","0.8333... The 3 repeats forever after the 8. Or: 5/6 as 5 × 1/6, and 1/6 is 0.1666... (8.NS.A.1)","5/9. For example: x = 0.555..., 10x = 5.555..., 9x = 5, x = 5/9. Or: name it from the pattern, since 0.111... is 1/9 so 0.555... is 5/9. Partial: 5/9 with no steps shown is approaching. (8.NS.A.1)"]},{title:"Squares and Cubes",std:["8.EE.A.2"],dur:100,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",qs:["Solve x<sup>2</sup> = 49. Give every solution.","Solve x<sup>3</sup> = 27.","Evaluate √169 and ∛216.","A square patch of cleared ice has an area of 5 square meters. Write its exact side length. Is that number rational or irrational?"],key:["x = 7 and x = −7, or ±√49. Partial: x = 7 only leaves the negative root out and is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","x = 3, or ∛27. Or: list the cubes to 27 instead of taking a cube root. Note that a cube root has one real solution, unlike a square root. (8.EE.A.2)","13 and 6. Partial: one of the two right is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","√5 meters. Irrational. Partial: 2.24 meters is a decimal approximation, not the exact side, and is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Powder Powers",short:"Exponents",parts:[{std:["8.EE.A.1"],dur:110,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",qs:["Write 3<sup>2</sup> × 3<sup>5</sup> as a single power of 3.","Write 7<sup>5</sup> ÷ 7<sup>3</sup> as a single power of 7. Then find its value.","Write (2<sup>3</sup>)<sup>4</sup> as a single power of 2.","What is the value of 12<sup>0</sup>?","Simplify 5<sup>2</sup> × 5<sup>−4</sup>. Write your answer as a fraction with no negative exponent."],key:["3<sup>7</sup> (or 2,187). Or: write both out as factors and count seven 3s. Partial: 3¹⁰ comes from multiplying the exponents and does not count. (8.EE.A.1)","7<sup>2</sup> = 49. Or: cancel three 7s from the factor strings. Partial: the power right with the value wrong is approaching. (8.EE.A.1)","2<sup>12</sup> (or 4,096). Or: write (2³)(2³)(2³)(2³) and add the exponents. Partial: 2⁷ comes from adding instead of multiplying and does not count. (8.EE.A.1)","1 (8.EE.A.1)","5<sup>−2</sup> = 1/5<sup>2</sup> = 1/25. Or: 25 ÷ 625 = 1/25 worked out as numbers. Partial: 5⁻² left with a negative exponent is approaching. (8.EE.A.1)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Snowfall Rate",short:"Rates",parts:[{std:["8.EE.B.5"],dur:140,dir:"Look at the graph. Answer out loud. Sketch number 3 on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<p class="big">During a storm, snow piles up on the ground and gets deeper and deeper. Two weather stations measured how deep it got.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${snowGraph()}</div>`,qs:["The graph shows Station A. What is the slope of the line? What does the slope mean in this situation?","Station B is up the valley. Its snow depth is s = 2.5h, where s is centimeters and h is hours. Which station got snow faster? How do you know?","On your paper, sketch the graph of Station B from 0 to 6 hours. Name two points on your line."],key:["Slope is 4. Station A got 4 centimeters of snow per hour. The slope is the unit rate. Or: read the rise over the run between any two points on the line. Partial: 4 given with no account of what it means in centimeters an hour is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)","Station A. Its unit rate is 4 centimeters per hour and Station B’s is 2.5 centimeters per hour. Compares a graph to an equation. Or: graph Station B on the same axes and compare the steepness. Partial: the right station with no two rates compared is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)","A straight line through (0, 0) and (6, 15). Other correct points: (2, 5), (4, 10), (1, 2.5). Partial: a correct line with only one point named is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Two Plow Plans",short:"Plow Plans",parts:[{std:["8.EE.C.8"],dur:210,dir:"Use the graph for number 1. Solve the rest on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Birchfold must pick one way to pay for plowing. Plan A pays a contractor for each storm. Plan B leases a village plow truck.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${planGraph()}</div>`,qs:["At what point do the two lines cross? What does that point mean for the village?","Solve this system with algebra: y = 3x − 4 and y = −x + 8.","Without solving, tell how many solutions each system has and how you know. (a) y = −4x + 2 and y = −4x + 9. (b) y = 2x − 5 and 2y = 4x − 10.","The warming hut sold 25 drinks in one afternoon and took in $86. Hot cocoa costs $4 and tea costs $2. How many of each did it sell?"],key:["(5, 20). After 5 storms both plans have cost the same, 20 hundreds of dollars, which is $2,000. After 5 storms Plan B costs less. Partial: the crossing point read right with no account of what it means is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","x = 3, y = 5, or (3, 5). Check: 3(3) − 4 = 5 and −3 + 8 = 5. Or: substitution, elimination, or setting the two expressions equal all count; setting 3x − 4 = −x + 8 is the quickest here. Partial: x = 3 with no y is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","(a) No solution. Same slope −4 and different y-intercepts, so the lines are parallel and never cross. (b) Infinitely many solutions. Divide the second equation by 2 and it is y = 2x − 5, the same line. Or: subtract the equations, which gives 0 = 7 for (a) and 0 = 0 for (b). Partial: one of the two systems answered is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","18 cups of cocoa and 7 cups of tea. c + t = 25 and 4c + 2t = 86. Check: 18 + 7 = 25 and 72 + 14 = 86. Or: substitution, elimination, or guess and check against both conditions all count. Partial: a correct pair of equations with the solving unfinished is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Rental Rules",short:"Functions",parts:[{title:"Is It a Function?",std:["8.F.A.1"],dur:100,dir:"Look at the tables and graphs. Answer out loud.",fig:()=>funcTables()+funcGraphs(),qs:["Which table shows a function, Table A or Table B? How do you know?","Which graph shows a function, Graph P or Graph Q? How can you tell?","A function’s rule is “multiply the input by 3, then subtract 4.” Name two ordered pairs that are on its graph."],key:["Table A. Each input has exactly one output. In Table B the input 5 has two outputs, 4 and 9. Partial: the right table named with no account of the repeated input is approaching. (8.F.A.1)","Graph P. Each x-value has one y-value. Graph Q is a circle, so some x-values have two y-values. At x = 0 the circle gives both 3 and −3, and a vertical line would cross it twice. Or: naming the vertical line test counts as the reason. (8.F.A.1)","Any two pairs that fit y = 3x − 4, such as (0, −4), (1, −1), (2, 2), (3, 5). (8.F.A.1)"]},{title:"Linear or Not",std:["8.F.A.3"],dur:100,dir:"Answer out loud. Use your paper if you need it.",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="text-align:center">y = 4x − 3 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; y = x<sup>2</sup> − 2 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; y = −<sup>1</sup>⁄<sub>3</sub>x + 6 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; y = 2<sup>x</sup></p>`,qs:["In y = −5x + 4, what do the −5 and the 4 tell you about the graph?","Which of the four equations above are linear functions? How can you tell?","The volume of a cube is V = s<sup>3</sup>. Its graph has the points (1, 1), (2, 8), and (3, 27). Use those points to explain why this function is not linear."],key:["−5 is the slope (rate of change), so the line goes down 5 for every 1 to the right. 4 is the y-intercept, where the line crosses the y-axis. The graph is a straight line. Partial: one of the two numbers explained is approaching. (8.F.A.3)","y = 4x − 3 and y = −⅓x + 6. They fit y = mx + b. The other two do not: one has x squared and one has x as the exponent, so their graphs curve. Or: tabling each one and checking for a constant step counts as the reason. Partial: the right two named with no reason is approaching. (8.F.A.3)","The change is not constant. From 1 to 8 is +7, and from 8 to 27 is +19, so the points are not on a straight line. Or: find the slope between two pairs of points and show the two slopes differ. (8.F.A.3)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Sled Slide Moves",short:"Moves",parts:[{std:["8.G.A.3"],dur:160,dir:"Start from the original triangle DEF each time. Tell your teacher the new coordinates.",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${sledGrid()}</div>`,qs:["Translate triangle DEF 7 units left and 3 units down. What are the new coordinates of D, E, and F?","Reflect triangle DEF across the x-axis. What are the new coordinates? What happened to the y-coordinates?","Rotate triangle DEF 90° clockwise around the origin. Where does point E land?","Dilate triangle DEF by a scale factor of 2 from the origin. What are the new coordinates? What happens to the side lengths?"],key:["D′(−6, −1), E′(−2, −2), F′(−4, 2). Subtract 7 from each x and 3 from each y. Or: count the squares on the grid instead of using the rule. Partial: two of the three points right is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","D′(1, −2), E′(5, −1), F′(3, −5). The y-coordinates change sign. Partial: the three points right with nothing said about the y-coordinates is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","E′(1, −5). The rule is (x, y) → (y, −x). Full triangle: D′(2, −1), F′(5, −3). Or: trace the triangle and turn the paper a quarter turn, or call it a 270° counterclockwise turn. Partial: E′ right with no rule named is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","D′(2, 4), E′(10, 2), F′(6, 10). Each coordinate doubles and each side is twice as long. Partial: the three points right with nothing said about the side lengths is approaching. (8.G.A.3)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Snowshoe Triangles",short:"Triangles",parts:[{std:["8.G.B.7"],dur:150,dir:"Solve on your paper. Show your work and hold it up.",fig:()=>triFigs(),qs:["Find the length of side c in the sled run triangle.","A wire 17 feet long runs from the ground to a point on a trail sign post. It is anchored 8 feet from the base of the post. How high up the post is the wire attached?","A rental crate is 12 inches long, 8 inches wide, and 9 inches tall. What is the longest straight trail marker rod that fits inside, from one bottom corner to the opposite top corner?"],key:["25 m. 15² + 20² = 225 + 400 = 625, and √625 = 25. Or: recognise the 3, 4, 5 triple scaled by 5. Partial: 625 found with no square root taken is approaching. (8.G.B.7)","15 feet. 17² − 8² = 289 − 64 = 225, and √225 = 15. Or: recognise the 8, 15, 17 triple. Partial: adding instead of subtracting, giving √353, does not count. (8.G.B.7)","17 inches. 12² + 8² + 9² = 144 + 64 + 81 = 289, and √289 = 17. May also find the floor diagonal first, √208. (8.G.B.7, three dimensions)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Snow and Sleds",short:"Scatter",parts:[{std:["8.SP.A.1"],dur:140,dir:"Look at the scatter plot. Answer out loud. Do number 4 on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<p class="big">The sled rental wrote down how deep the snow was on the ground each day and how many sleds it rented. Deeper snow means a bigger number of centimeters.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${scatter()}</div>`,qs:["Describe the pattern in the 12 days of data. Is the association positive or negative? Is it linear or nonlinear?","One point does not fit the pattern. What are its coordinates? Give one possible reason for it.","About how many sleds would you expect the shop to rent on a day with 14 centimeters of snow on the ground? Explain.","On your paper, make a scatter plot of these four new days: (4, 5), (14, 18), (23, 30), (31, 42). Do they follow the same pattern?"],key:["Positive and roughly linear. Deeper snow on the ground goes with more sleds rented. Partial: positive named with nothing said about linear, or the reverse, is approaching. (8.SP.A.1)","(24, 9). An outlier. Possible reasons: the hill road was closed that day, a storm kept people home, hard wind or deep cold, or the shop closed early. Partial: the point named with no possible reason is approaching. (8.SP.A.1)","About 17 or 18 sleds. It falls between (12, 15) and (16, 20) on the upward trend. Accept 16 to 20. Or: draw a trend line and read off its value at 14. Partial: a number in range with no account of the trend is approaching. (8.SP.A.1)","Plots the four points correctly with labeled axes. Yes, the same positive, roughly linear pattern. Partial: the four points plotted with no answer about the pattern is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, construct)"]}]},{id:15,title:"What the Declaration Says",short:"Declaration",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.1"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the structure and argument of the Declaration of Independence",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Declaration of Independence has three parts that do three different jobs.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
     <tr><td>The opening</td><td>states that all men are created equal, hold unalienable rights, and that governments get their just powers from the consent of the governed</td></tr>
     <tr><td>The long middle</td><td>lists twenty seven specific charges against the king: taxes without consent, dissolving colonial assemblies, quartering troops, denying trial by jury</td></tr>
     <tr><td>The close</td><td>declares the colonies free and independent states, and pledges the signers to it</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("lantern",74)}${ico("flag",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why open with a general claim about rights before listing any complaint about the king?","The middle section is the longest by far. Who was it written for?","The opening says a people may alter or abolish a government that fails them. Explain how that sentence is both the heart of the argument and a risky thing to write down."],key:["Because the general claim is the standard the complaints are measured against. Without it the list is just grievances, which any subject might have. With it, the list becomes proof that this government has broken the only terms on which it could rightly govern, so separation is a remedy rather than a rebellion. Partial: naming the grievances with nothing said about the standard they are measured against is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","For an audience that had to be convinced: other colonists who were undecided, and foreign governments, especially France, whose help the colonies needed. Specific, checkable charges made the case look like a legal indictment rather than a complaint, which is what persuades a cautious reader or a possible ally. Partial: one audience named is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","It is the heart of the argument because it supplies the right the signers were exercising: if authority comes from consent, consent can be withdrawn. It is risky because the same sentence can be turned on any later government, including their own, and it was: enslaved people, women, and secessionists all later quoted it back. A principle cannot be written to apply only once. Partial: one of the strength and the risk named is approaching. (HSS-8.1)"]},{std:["HSS-8.2"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing checks and balances and locating the enumerated and implied powers at work",html:()=>`<p class="big">Each row is one branch acting on another.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Branch acting</th><th>What it can do</th><th>On whom</th></tr>
     <tr><td>President</td><td>veto a bill</td><td>Congress</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Congress</td><td>override a veto with two thirds of each house</td><td>President</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Senate</td><td>refuse to confirm a nominee</td><td>President</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Congress</td><td>impeach and remove an officer</td><td>President or judges</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Supreme Court</td><td>hold a law unconstitutional</td><td>Congress and the states</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("snowflake",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Four of these five powers are written in the Constitution and one is not. Which is the odd one out, and where does it come from?","The override needs two thirds rather than a simple majority. What is that number doing?","Pick any one row and describe what could go wrong if that check did not exist."],key:["Holding a law unconstitutional. Judicial review is not in the text; the Court claimed it in Marbury v. Madison in 1803, reasoning from the Constitution being the supreme law and from the judicial power to decide cases. It is the clearest example of an implied power outside Congress. Partial: naming the power with nothing said about where the Court got it is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","It makes overriding hard on purpose. A simple majority already passed the bill once, so a simple majority override would make the veto meaningless. Two thirds means Congress can defeat a president who is out of step with a broad consensus, but cannot brush aside an objection it only narrowly disagrees with. Partial: naming the fraction with no account of why a simple majority would not do is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","Any row with a sensible consequence. Without the veto, Congress alone decides and a narrow majority faces no obstacle. Without confirmation, one person staffs an entire branch. Without impeachment, an officer who commits crimes stays until the term ends. Without judicial review, a law stands however plainly it violates the Constitution. Without the override, a single president can block any law at all. Partial: a row named with no consequence of removing it is approaching. (HSS-8.2)"]},{std:["HSS-8.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing local government and the points at which a resident can take part",html:()=>`<p class="big">Decisions in a small village, and who makes each one.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Who decides</th><th>Chosen how</th></tr>
     <tr><td>what the elementary school teaches and spends</td><td>the school board</td><td>elected by district voters</td></tr>
     <tr><td>whether to buy a new plow truck</td><td>the village council</td><td>elected by village voters</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the property tax rate</td><td>the village council, often at an open meeting</td><td>elected</td></tr>
     <tr><td>whether a road is a state highway</td><td>the state department of transportation</td><td>appointed officials</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("cabin",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Three of these decision makers are elected and one is appointed. If a resident disagrees with the appointed officials, what can she do?","School board elections usually draw far fewer voters than presidential elections, though they decide what happens to local children every day. Why might that be, and what is the consequence?","Name two things that make a local decision easier for one resident to influence than a national one."],key:["Go through the elected officials who oversee them: contact her state legislator and the governor's office, testify at a public hearing the department holds, or vote in the state elections that decide who appoints the department's head. Appointed officials are reached indirectly, through the elected people responsible for them. Partial: naming a complaint to the department with no elected route named is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Local races get little news coverage, are often held on their own dates, and have no national campaign driving turnout, so many voters do not know a race is happening or who is running. The consequence is that a small, organized group can decide the outcome, which gives a resident who does show up unusual influence. Partial: naming the low turnout with nothing said about what it means for a voter who does show up is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Any two: far fewer voters, so one vote and one voice weigh more; officials who live nearby and can be reached in person; meetings open to the public where anyone may speak before the vote; a smaller agenda, so one issue is not buried; and the fact that most local bodies must publish their budgets and their meeting notices. Partial: one reason named is approaching. (HSS-8.3)"]}]},{id:16,title:"An American Style",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the search for a distinctly American culture as an aspiration of the new nation",html:()=>`<p class="big">After independence, Americans kept noticing that their books, buildings and paintings were still European. Washington Irving and James Fenimore Cooper wrote stories set in the Hudson Valley and on the frontier instead of in London drawing rooms. Painters of the Hudson River School made enormous canvases of American mountains and rivers, arguing that a young country's grandeur was in its landscape rather than its ruins. Public buildings were put up with columns and domes borrowed from the Roman Republic, which Americans saw as the one earlier government worth claiming kinship with.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pine",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("palette",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why would a new country care what its novels and paintings were about?","European cities had cathedrals, castles and ruins centuries old. What did American painters offer instead, and what were they claiming by it?","American public buildings copied Roman forms rather than inventing new ones. Is that a contradiction? Defend your answer."],key:["Because political independence does not by itself make a people distinct, and Americans were conscious of being treated as provincial imitators of Britain. A literature and art with American subjects was a claim that the country had experiences and places of its own worth writing about, which is a claim about being a nation and not just a former colony. Partial: naming the new writing with nothing said about the claim to being a nation is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","Mountains, forests, rivers and light, painted at a scale usually reserved for history and heroes. The claim was that the country's greatness lay in what it had rather than in what it lacked, and that a landscape untouched by old empires was itself a kind of promise. It also quietly erased the Native nations who already lived in those landscapes. Partial: describing the paintings with nothing said about the claim they made is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","Any defended answer. For a contradiction: copying another civilization's forms is an odd way to prove you have your own. Against: they chose the Roman Republic specifically, not a monarchy, so the borrowing was an argument about what kind of government the building housed. A strong answer notices that the choice of model was itself the statement. Partial: a verdict with no reasoning is approaching. (HSS-8.4)"]},{std:["HSS-8.5"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the dispute with France in the late 1790s and how it was settled",html:()=>`<p class="big">After Jay's treaty with Britain, France began seizing American merchant ships. In 1797 President John Adams sent three envoys to Paris. French officials refused to negotiate unless the envoys paid a bribe and arranged a large loan. When Adams published the dispatches, with the French agents' names replaced by the letters X, Y and Z, the country was furious. Congress built warships, created a Marine Corps, and authorized American vessels to fight French ones at sea, but war was never declared. In 1800 Adams sent another mission and settled the quarrel by treaty, which cost him much of his own party's support.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sailboat",74)}${ico("anchor",74)}${ico("globe",74)}</div>`,qs:["Adams could have asked Congress for a declaration of war and probably got one. Why do you think he did not?","What did publishing the dispatches accomplish for Adams at home?","Name one lasting result of this episode."],key:["Because a full war with France would have cost far more than the country could afford, risked the merchant fleet the economy ran on, and would have put the United States effectively on Britain's side, which is exactly the entanglement Washington had warned against. He judged a limited naval fight and then a settlement served the country better than a popular war. Partial: one reason named is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","It turned a diplomatic failure into evidence of French insult, which united public opinion behind his government and made the naval buildup easy to fund. It is an early example of a president using the release of documents to shape opinion, and it worked so well that it later made his own peace settlement look like a betrayal. Partial: naming the publication with nothing said about what it did to opinion is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","Any accurate result: a permanent United States Navy and Marine Corps; the 1800 treaty that ended the 1778 alliance with France and left the country formally unentangled; the Alien and Sedition Acts passed in the same alarm, which became a major argument about free speech; and the split in Adams's own party that helped cost him the 1800 election. Partial: a result asserted with no link to the crisis is approaching. (HSS-8.5)"]},{std:["HSS-8.6"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the transportation revolution in the Northeast and its effect on prices and markets",html:()=>`<p class="big">How long it took to send goods from New York City to Cincinnati.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Best route</th><th>Travel time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1817</td><td>wagon over turnpikes and river boat</td><td>about 50 days</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1840</td><td>canal and steamboat</td><td>about 18 days</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1860</td><td>railroad</td><td>about 6 to 8 days</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Steamboats mattered most going upstream: before them, a boat that floated down the Ohio was usually broken up for lumber at the end of the trip.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("truck",74)}${ico("canoe",74)}${ico("bridge",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why going upstream was the hard problem, and what the steamboat changed about it.","Faster shipping lowered what things cost in distant cities. Name one group that gained and one that lost from that.","Railroads could run where canals could not. Name two advantages that gave them."],key:["Current. Floating downstream needed only steering, so goods moved cheaply one way, but hauling a loaded boat back up against the Mississippi or the Ohio was slow, expensive and often not worth doing. A steam engine made the return trip practical, which turned a one way chute into a two way route and let the interior buy as well as sell. Partial: naming the current with nothing said about the return trip is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Gained: Eastern city consumers, who got cheaper flour, pork and lumber; Western farmers, who finally had a market worth planting for; and merchants and manufacturers who could now sell nationally. Lost: New England farmers who could not match Western prices, and the teamsters, innkeepers and turnpike companies whose business the new routes bypassed. Partial: one of the gainers and the losers named is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Any two: they ran in winter when canals froze; they went over hills and across country where water would not go, so a town did not have to sit on a river; they were much faster; and they could be built almost anywhere there was capital, which meant a place could get a connection by raising money rather than by luck of geography. Partial: one advantage named is approaching. (HSS-8.6)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Three Regions, Three Pressures",short:"Sections",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.7"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing how enslaved people in the South acted for themselves within and against the system",html:()=>`<p class="big">People held in slavery were not passive. They slowed work, broke tools and feigned illness to limit what could be taken from them. They kept family lines and histories in memory across sales, and named children for grandparents they might never see again. They learned to read in secret, taught each other, and passed news along the roads by word of mouth. Thousands ran away, some for a few days to force a change in treatment and some for good. A few organized revolts, including one led by Denmark Vesey in Charleston in 1822 and one led by Nat Turner in Virginia in 1831, and Southern legislatures answered both with tighter restrictions.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hands",74)}${ico("lantern",74)}${ico("pine",74)}</div>`,qs:["Slowing work and breaking tools look small next to a revolt. Why were they the most common form of resistance?","Keeping family history in memory was deliberate work. Why did it matter so much?","Southern states responded to the 1831 revolt by restricting Black southerners further, including free ones. What does that reaction tell you about the system's own position?"],key:["Because they were survivable. An open revolt was almost certain to fail against armed patrols and militia and brought severe punishment to many people who had no part in it, while a slow pace or a broken plow was hard to prove and hard to punish individually. It let people reduce what was extracted from them and keep some control over their own days. Partial: naming the quiet resistance with nothing said about why open revolt was not survivable is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","Because sale could separate a family at any moment and the law recorded none of it: no marriage, no legal father, often no surname. Memory was the only record that existed, so remembering who your grandparents were and where they went was how a family stayed a family, and it is how many people found each other after 1865. Partial: naming the separations with nothing said about memory being the only record is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","That it was held in place by force and knew it. If slavery had rested on the contentment its defenders claimed, a single revolt would not have produced new laws against gathering, preaching, teaching, traveling and even against free Black people living in the state. Tightening restrictions after every scare is the behavior of a system that expected resistance. Partial: listing the new laws with nothing said about what they reveal is approaching. (HSS-8.7)"]},{std:["HSS-8.8"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the war with Mexico, the territory it transferred and what it left unsettled",html:()=>`<p class="big">The war with Mexico and its settlement.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th></th></tr>
     <tr><td>Dates</td><td>1846 to 1848</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Dispute that started it</td><td>whether the Texas border was the Nueces River or the Rio Grande, and American offers to buy California that Mexico refused</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Treaty</td><td>Guadalupe Hidalgo, 1848</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Land transferred</td><td>about 525,000 square miles: California, Nevada, Utah, most of Arizona and New Mexico, parts of Colorado and Wyoming</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Paid to Mexico</td><td>$15 million, plus assumption of claims</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Promised to Mexicans who stayed</td><td>citizenship and protection of their property</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["A boundary dispute does not usually end with half a million square miles changing hands. What was the war actually about?","The treaty promised Mexican landowners their property. Why did many lose it anyway?","Some members of Congress opposed this war at the time. On what grounds would an opponent in 1847 have argued against it?"],key:["Territory, chiefly California and its Pacific harbors, which the United States had already tried to buy. The border question gave a reason to send troops where a clash was likely, and the war then produced by conquest what purchase had failed to get. The size of the transfer is the clearest evidence of the aim. Partial: naming the border dispute as the aim is approaching, since the territory is what the war delivered. (HSS-8.8)","Because proving a title under the new system required documents, English, lawyers and years in American courts, while squatters moved onto the land and new state laws and taxes favored the newcomers. Many families won in court and were ruined by the cost, or lost on technicalities in records kept under a different legal tradition. A promise in a treaty is only as good as the procedure for enforcing it. Partial: noting the lost land with nothing said about the procedure for enforcing a title is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","Any supported argument: that the president had provoked the clash and presented Congress with a war already begun; that conquering a neighbor contradicted the country's stated principles; and above all that any territory taken would reopen the fight over whether slavery could expand, which is precisely what happened. Partial: an objection named with no support is approaching. (HSS-8.8)"]},{std:["HSS-8.9"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing immediate abolition as an argument and the organizing that carried it",html:()=>`<p class="big">On the first day of 1831 William Lloyd Garrison printed the first issue of The Liberator in Boston and announced he would not ask for gradual abolition. Slavery was a sin, he argued, and a sin is not reduced by instalments, so it should end now and without payment to owners. That reasoning came out of the religious revivals then sweeping the North, which taught that a converted person renounces a sin at once and works to end it in others. Two years later he helped found the American Anti-Slavery Society, which by 1838 had well over a thousand local chapters and a quarter of a million members, many of them organized through Protestant congregations and through the independent Black churches of Northern cities. The society flooded the mail with pamphlets, sent speakers town to town, and gathered signatures by the hundred thousand for petitions to Congress.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("torch",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["What is the difference between gradual and immediate abolition, and why would Garrison refuse the gradual version?","Calling slavery a sin rather than only a bad policy changed what the argument demanded. Explain how.","The society built a thousand local chapters, many of them in congregations, instead of lobbying in Washington alone. Why organize that way?"],key:["Gradual abolition ends slavery over decades, usually by freeing future children at adulthood and often by compensating owners. Immediate abolition ends it at once and treats owners as having no rightful claim. Garrison refused the gradual version because it accepted that holding people as property was legitimate for now, and because every year of delay meant more people born into it. Partial: defining the two with no account of why he refused the gradual version is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","A bad policy can be weighed against other interests, phased out, or paid for. A sin, in the revival preaching of the 1830s, had to be renounced immediately and completely, and anyone who tolerated it shared in it. That framing made compromise itself the thing to be condemned, it put pressure on individual consciences rather than only on Congress, and it is why defenders of slavery answered with religious arguments of their own. Partial: naming the religious language with nothing said about what it does to compromise is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","Because Congress would not act until public opinion forced it, and opinion is changed locally: a neighbor who speaks at a meeting persuades where a distant petition does not. Congregations already met weekly, already had officers and collections, and already discussed right conduct, so they were the readiest organizing structure in the country. The independent Black churches of Philadelphia, New York and Boston were centers of the movement for the same reason. Partial: naming the churches with nothing said about why they were the readiest structure is approaching. (HSS-8.9)"]}]},{id:18,title:"War, Freedom, Cities",short:"War and After",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.10"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Emancipation Proclamation, its limits and its effects",html:()=>`<p class="big">What the Emancipation Proclamation of January 1, 1863 did and did not reach.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>Covered?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>states in rebellion</td><td>yes, declared free</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Kentucky, Maryland, Missouri, Delaware, which stayed in the Union</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>parts of Louisiana and Virginia already under Union control</td><td>no</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">It also authorized the enlistment of Black soldiers. By the end of the war about 180,000 had served in the United States Army and about 19,000 in its Navy.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("key",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["It freed people in places the Union did not yet control and left out places it did. Explain that, rather than calling it a mistake.","Why does a document with those limits still count as a turning point?","Black soldiers were at first paid less than white soldiers for the same service. Why would men enlist anyway, and what did many of them do about the pay?"],key:["It was issued as a war measure under the president's authority as commander in chief, so it could only reach territory in rebellion. Freeing people in loyal slave states would have required Congress or an amendment, and Lincoln judged that pushing those states further would risk losing them. The limits are the shape of his legal authority, not an oversight. Partial: naming the limits with nothing said about the authority he was acting under is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Because it changed what a Union victory would mean: restoring the Union now meant ending slavery in the rebel states, which made any negotiated peace that preserved slavery impossible. It also made Britain and France far less likely to recognize the Confederacy, and it opened the army to Black soldiers, which added manpower the Confederacy could not match. Partial: one consequence named is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Many enlisted because the war was now explicitly about ending slavery and because service was a claim on citizenship that would be hard to deny afterward. Soldiers of the 54th Massachusetts and other regiments refused to accept the unequal pay at all, serving without wages until Congress equalized it in 1864. Partial: naming the enlistment with nothing said about the unequal pay or the claim on citizenship is approaching. (HSS-8.10)"]},{std:["HSS-8.11"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Black officeholding during Reconstruction and what those governments accomplished",html:()=>`<p class="big">Once the Fifteenth Amendment and federal enforcement were in place, Black southerners voted in large numbers and held office. Sixteen served in Congress between 1870 and 1877, including Hiram Revels and Blanche Bruce of Mississippi in the United States Senate. More than six hundred served in state legislatures, and hundreds more as sheriffs, county clerks, justices of the peace and school board members. The legislatures they sat in wrote the South's first state constitutions guaranteeing public schools for all children, abolished imprisonment for debt, and rebuilt roads, bridges and hospitals.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["These were the first public school systems in most Southern states. Why had the South built so few before the war?","Why did it matter that Black southerners held local offices like sheriff and justice of the peace, and not only seats in Congress?","These officeholders were voted out or driven from office within about a decade. Name the main reason, and say what remained."],key:["Because a plantation economy run by a small wealthy class saw little reason to tax itself to educate other people's children, and because teaching enslaved children to read was illegal. Public schooling requires a public that expects to be included, and Reconstruction created one. White children in the South gained from these systems too. Partial: naming the absence of schools with nothing said about who gained from the new systems is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Because those are the offices that touch a person's life: a sheriff decides who is arrested, a justice of the peace decides small cases, a clerk records a deed or a marriage, a school board hires teachers. National seats are symbolically large, but local office is where the law is actually applied, which is also why those offices were the first targets when the counter campaign began. Partial: naming the offices with nothing said about where the law is actually applied is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","The reason was the withdrawal of federal enforcement, agreed around the disputed election of 1876 and carried out in 1877, after which organized intimidation at the polls and new voting restrictions went unchecked. What remained were the three amendments, the state constitutions' school provisions, and the plain historical fact that these governments had functioned, which later generations used as evidence against the claim that they never had. Partial: one of the reason and what remained is approaching. (HSS-8.11)"]},{std:["HSS-8.12"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing rapid urban growth and the changing social conditions that came with it",html:()=>`<p class="big">City populations, in thousands.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>City</th><th>1860</th><th>1900</th></tr>
     <tr><td>New York</td><td>1,175</td><td>3,437</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Chicago</td><td>112</td><td>1,699</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Detroit</td><td>46</td><td>286</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">In 1860 about one American in five lived in a city. By 1900 it was about two in five. New tenement buildings packed families into rooms without windows until city laws required them; streetcars, then electric lights, then piped water and sewers followed, and the wealthier residents moved out along the streetcar lines.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("box",74)}${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("bus",74)}</div>`,qs:["Chicago grew more than fifteenfold in forty years. Where did those people come from?","Why would tenements get built without windows in every room, and what finally changed it?","Streetcars let people live further from work. Explain how that changed who lived where in a city."],key:["From three sources: immigrants arriving from Europe, Americans leaving farms as machinery reduced the labor a farm needed, and Black southerners beginning to move north. Chicago in particular pulled people in because the rail network met there, so meatpacking, grain handling and manufacturing concentrated around it. Partial: one or two of the three sources named is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Because landlords were paid by the room and the demand was desperate, so filling every square foot was profitable and no law said otherwise. What changed it was regulation: housing laws requiring windows, air shafts and plumbing, pushed by inspectors, journalists and reformers who documented the conditions. It is a case of a market outcome being altered by politics. Partial: naming the conditions with nothing said about what changed them is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","It sorted the city by income. Anyone who could afford the daily fare and a house further out moved there, while the people who could not stayed in the crowded center near the work. That is the beginning of the pattern of better off neighborhoods on the edge and the poorest housing in the middle, and it changed who had a say in how each part of the city was run. Partial: naming the move outward with nothing said about who could not afford it is approaching. (HSS-8.12)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Wax on a Spruce Needle",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing a change in a gene through the protein it codes for to the trait that results",html:()=>`<p class="big">The spruces above the village carry four versions of one gene. It codes for an enzyme that lays down the waxy coat on a needle, and the coat is what keeps the needle from drying out in hard frost.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Gene version</th><th>Protein the gene codes for</th><th>Needles survive down to</th></tr>
     <tr><td>W1, unchanged</td><td>full length enzyme, normal amount, thick wax laid down</td><td>minus 35 degrees Celsius</td></tr>
     <tr><td>W2, one base changed in the folding region</td><td>full length protein that folds wrongly and is taken apart by the cell</td><td>minus 12 degrees Celsius</td></tr>
     <tr><td>W3, three bases removed, one amino acid missing</td><td>enzyme folds and works at half the normal rate</td><td>minus 22 degrees Celsius</td></tr>
     <tr><td>W4, change in a stretch cut out before the protein is built</td><td>the same enzyme, the same amount</td><td>minus 35 degrees Celsius</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pine",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}</div>`,qs:["W2 carries a full set of instructions and the tree still has almost no wax. Say exactly where the chain from gene to trait breaks.","Put W1, W3 and W2 in order of wax thickness and tie each one to its protein.","A seedling with W2 is raised in a warm greenhouse all its life and its needles stay green. A grower says that proves the wax gene does not matter. Explain what is wrong with that."],key:["The gene is read and a full length protein is built, so the break is not in reading the gene. The changed base sits where the chain folds, the protein takes the wrong shape, and the cell destroys misfolded protein, so no working enzyme survives. With no enzyme there is no wax laid down, and without wax the needle loses water in hard frost and dies at minus 12 instead of minus 35. The break is at folding, between building the protein and the protein doing its job. Partial: saying no working enzyme survives, with the break not placed at the folding step between building the protein and the protein doing its job, is approaching. (LS3)","Thickest first: W1 with a full rate enzyme lays thick wax and holds to minus 35. W3 with a half rate enzyme lays thinner wax and holds to minus 22. W2 with no surviving enzyme lays almost none and fails at minus 12. The trait tracks how much working enzyme the cell ends up with, so a partly working protein gives a partly expressed trait. Partial: the three versions put in the right order with the enzyme rate not tied to each one is approaching. (LS3)","The greenhouse removed the test, not the defect. The seedling still carries W2 and still builds no working enzyme, so it still has almost no wax; nothing ever got cold enough for that to matter. A trait is only visible when conditions call on it, so a green needle in a warm house is no evidence at all about what the gene does. Put the same seedling outside at minus 20 and the difference appears. Partial: saying the greenhouse was warm, with nothing said about the seedling still building no working enzyme, is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:20,title:"The Road Cut Above the Village",short:"Evolution",parts:[{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading the plant fossil sequence and structural homology as evidence of common ancestry",html:()=>`<p class="big">A new road above the village cut through the hillside. Layers pile up newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Rock</th><th>Plant fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1, 0 to 2 m</td><td>peat</td><td>spruce and birch pollen, the trees in the valley today</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2, 2 to 9 m</td><td>silt</td><td>oak and hickory pollen, trees of a much warmer climate</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3, 9 to 19 m</td><td>shale</td><td>seed fern leaves, and no flowering plant anywhere in the layer</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4, 19 to 30 m</td><td>coal</td><td>club mosses and horsetails 20 m tall, spores only, no seeds at all</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Five structures that all grow from the place on a stem where a leaf grows.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plant</th><th>What the structure looks like</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
     <tr><td>cactus</td><td>a stiff spine</td><td>guards the stem and loses almost no water</td></tr>
     <tr><td>pea</td><td>a thin coiling tendril</td><td>grips a support</td></tr>
     <tr><td>onion</td><td>a thick white scale</td><td>stores food underground</td></tr>
     <tr><td>pitcher plant</td><td>a deep water filled cup</td><td>traps insects</td></tr>
     <tr><td>spruce</td><td>a narrow waxy needle</td><td>photosynthesis in cold, dry wind</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pine",74)}${ico("cactus",74)}${ico("herb",74)}</div>`,qs:["Read the layers from oldest to youngest and describe what changes about how the plants reproduce.","Five structures that look nothing alike all grow in the position a leaf grows in. Explain what that tells you, and say why the position is what makes it an argument.","The earliest flowering plant fossils appear above the seed fern layer and never below it, anywhere in the world. Say what that adds to the first chart, and what it would take to overturn it."],key:["Oldest first: layer 4, giant club mosses and horsetails that reproduced by spores alone; layer 3, seed ferns, which reproduce by seed but have no flowers; layer 2, oak and hickory, flowering trees of a warm climate; layer 1, spruce and birch of the climate here now. Reproduction moves from spores, to seeds without flowers, to seeds inside flowers, each appearing after the one before. The sequence also records the climate changing, from a warm swamp to warm forest to cold forest. Partial: the four layers in the right order with nothing said about reproduction moving from spores to seeds to flowers is approaching. (LS4)","A spine, a tendril, a storage scale, a trap and a needle do completely unrelated jobs, so the job cannot be what they have in common. What they share is where and how they develop: each grows at a leaf position, from the same tissue, under the same developmental control. That is the signature of one inherited structure modified in different lineages, which is what common ancestry predicts, and it is why the position carries the argument while the appearance does not. Partial: saying all five grow where a leaf grows, with nothing said about the shared job not being what they have in common, is approaching. (LS4)","It turns the chart into a prediction that can fail. If forms appeared in no particular order, a flower would eventually turn up below a seed fern somewhere, and in millions of collected specimens it never has, so the order is a real feature of the record rather than an accident of one hillside. A single well dated flowering plant fossil found beneath undisturbed seed fern rock would overturn it, which is exactly what makes the claim scientific. Partial: saying the order holds everywhere, with nothing named that would overturn it, is approaching. (LS4)"]},{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both sets of numbers. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"explaining natural selection from a trait distribution and distinguishing it from artificial selection",html:()=>`<p class="big">Snowshoe hares live on the slope above the village. Snow now arrives about six weeks later than it did a century ago, so a white hare can spend weeks on brown ground where a hawk sees it easily. One hundred and fifty hares were scored each generation.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Winter coat</th><th>Generation 1</th><th>Generation 7</th><th>Generation 15</th></tr>
     <tr><td>turns fully white in October</td><td>96</td><td>61</td><td>23</td></tr>
     <tr><td>turns partly white</td><td>39</td><td>56</td><td>58</td></tr>
     <tr><td>stays brown all winter</td><td>15</td><td>33</td><td>69</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The village flock is a different story. For forty years the shepherds bred only from the ewes and rams carrying the heaviest fleece. Average fleece weight rose from 2.1 kg to 4.6 kg.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("rabbit",74)}${ico("sheep",74)}${ico("snowCloud",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why later snow moves the distribution the way it does. Name what varies, what the hawks do, and what is inherited.","Work out the share staying brown in generation 1 and in generation 15, then say what would happen to the distribution if the snow returned to its old dates.","Compare the hares with the village flock. Say who or what does the selecting in each case, and name one thing that selecting hard for a single trait can cost."],key:["When a hare turns white already varies in the population and is inherited. With snow arriving six weeks late, a hare that is white in October stands out against brown ground, so hawks take more of those and fewer of the ones that stay brown, which makes brown hares more likely to survive to breed. Their offspring inherit the gene versions for staying brown, so the share of brown hares climbs each generation. Variation, a difference in the probability of surviving and reproducing, then inheritance. Partial: two of the three steps given is approaching, and the inheritance step is usually the one left out. (LS4)","Generation 1: 15 of 150, which is 10 percent. Generation 15: 69 of 150, which is 46 percent. If the snow came back early, a brown hare would now be the one standing out against white ground, so the selection would reverse and the share of white coats would climb again. Natural selection has no direction of its own; it only follows whichever trait does better in the conditions of the moment. Partial: working out the 10 and 46 percent with no prediction made about the snow returning is approaching. (LS4)","The hawks and the timing of the snow do the selecting on the slope, with no one choosing anything. In the flock the shepherds choose the parents directly, which is artificial selection and moved fleece weight from 2.1 kg to 4.6 kg in forty years. Selecting hard for one trait narrows the variation in the whole flock, because breeding from a few animals drops gene versions that were not being looked at, and the flock can end up with less resistance to disease or harder lambing even as the fleece gets heavier. Partial: naming the selecting agent in both cases with nothing named that selecting hard for one trait can cost is approaching. (LS4)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Midwinter Sky",short:"Universe",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the record. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining daily and seasonal cycles from measurements and the part gravity plays in orbits",html:()=>`<p class="big">The village keeps a record of the midwinter sun and of one star.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Time</th><th>Height of the sun</th><th>Direction</th></tr>
     <tr><td>9:00 a.m.</td><td>2 degrees</td><td>southeast</td></tr>
     <tr><td>noon</td><td>14 degrees</td><td>south</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3:00 p.m.</td><td>2 degrees</td><td>southwest</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The star Polaris sits 48 degrees above the north horizon all night, every night of the year, while the other stars circle around it. In midsummer the noon sun reaches 61 degrees here.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("star",74)}${ico("cabin",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["The sun rises, crosses the sky and sets, and Polaris does not move at all. Explain both with one idea.","The noon sun reaches 14 degrees in midwinter and 61 degrees in midsummer here. Work out the difference and say what it tells you about Earth.","Earth travels around the sun at about 30 km each second and stays on its path. Name the force, then explain why a planet closer to the sun has to move faster to stay in orbit."],key:["Earth turns once a day on an axis. Everything in the sky appears to swing around that axis, so the sun traces an arc and sets, and so do most stars. Polaris happens to lie almost exactly along the direction the axis points, so turning Earth barely moves it at all and the other stars circle it. Its height above the north horizon equals the latitude of the village, which is why 48 degrees never changes here. Partial: saying Earth turns, with nothing said about Polaris lying along the direction the axis points, is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","61 minus 14 is 47 degrees, which is about twice 23.5. Earth's axis is tilted about 23.5 degrees to the plane of its orbit and keeps pointing the same way in space all year, so the noon sun swings 23.5 degrees above and 23.5 degrees below its equinox height, giving a total swing of about 47 degrees. The same tilt is what makes the midwinter sun low and the day short. Partial: working out the 47 degrees with nothing said about it being twice the 23.5 degree tilt is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Gravity. Closer to the sun the pull is stronger, so a planet needs a tighter, faster curve to balance it: at a lower speed the stronger pull would bend its path inward and it would spiral in, and at a higher speed it would swing out. That is why Mercury takes 88 days and Saturn takes 29 years, and why the inner planets all move faster than the outer ones. Partial: naming gravity with nothing said about a stronger pull needing a faster curve to balance it is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]},{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"comparing distances across the universe at scale and dating a layer against a rock carried into it",html:()=>`<p class="big">Distances given as the time light takes to cross them.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Object</th><th>Light travel time to it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>8.3 minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Proxima Centauri, the nearest other star</td><td>4.2 years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the center of our own galaxy</td><td>26,000 years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the Andromeda galaxy</td><td>2,500,000 years</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The road cut again, with the rock in each unit dated. A loose granite boulder sits in the peat at the top, dated at 1,100 million years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Unit, top to bottom</th><th>Age of the rock</th></tr>
     <tr><td>peat, with the loose granite boulder in it</td><td>10,000 years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>silt</td><td>5 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>shale</td><td>300 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>coal</td><td>320 million years</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("star",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["A year holds about 526,000 minutes. Work out roughly how many times farther away Proxima Centauri is than the sun.","A model puts the sun 1 m from Earth. Work out where Proxima Centauri goes, and say what the model shows about the gaps between stars.","The granite boulder is dated at 1,100 million years and sits in peat 10,000 years old. Explain how both dates can be right, and say what the boulder does and does not tell you."],key:["4.2 years is 4.2 times 526,000, which is about 2,210,000 light minutes. Divided by 8.3 minutes, that is about 266,000 times as far as the sun. The nearest other star is a quarter of a million times the distance to our own, which is why the sun looks like a disk and every other star looks like a point. Partial: getting the 2,210,000 light minutes with the division by 8.3 not carried out is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","If 8.3 light minutes is 1 m, then 1 m stands for 8.3 light minutes, and 2,210,000 light minutes needs 2,210,000 divided by 8.3, which is about 266,000 m, or 266 km. Earth to the sun fits on a table and the next star is a long drive away, so stars are separated by gaps vastly larger than the systems around them. Space between stars is mostly empty by an enormous margin. Partial: getting the 266 km with nothing said about the gaps between stars being far larger than the systems around them is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","A radiometric date gives the age of the rock itself, not the age of the place it is lying in. The granite crystallized 1,100 million years ago somewhere else, and ice carried the block here and dropped it in the peat within the last 10,000 years. The boulder tells you that granite of that age exists upstream along the path the ice took. It does not date the peat, because only rock that formed in place dates the layer it sits in. Partial: saying the ice carried the boulder there, with nothing said about a date giving the age of the rock rather than the place, is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Wood for the Winter",short:"Human Impact",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS3"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"separating population growth from per person consumption in total demand on a resource",html:()=>`<p class="big">Firewood in this village, from a hundred and twenty five years of village and county records. The forest around the village grows about 11,500 cubic metres of new wood a year.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>People</th><th>Cubic metres per person per year</th><th>Total cubic metres</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1900</td><td>1,200</td><td>9.0</td><td>10,800</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1950</td><td>2,000</td><td>6.5</td><td>13,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2000</td><td>3,400</td><td>3.2</td><td>10,880</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2025</td><td>5,000</td><td>2.2</td><td>11,000</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pine",74)}${ico("fire",74)}${ico("cabin",74)}</div>`,qs:["Per person use fell by more than three quarters and the total barely moved. Explain that with the numbers.","Suppose the village reaches 7,000 people by 2050 and the total has to stay under the forest's 11,500 cubic metres of yearly growth. Work out the per person figure that would require.","Name two effects on Earth's systems if the village cut more than 11,500 cubic metres a year for a long time. Then say what the chart shows. Can efficiency alone keep a growing village inside a fixed limit?"],key:["Per person use fell from 9.0 to 2.2 cubic metres, a factor of about 0.24, while the number of people rose from 1,200 to 5,000, a factor of about 4.17. Multiplied together, 4.17 times 0.24 is about 1.0, so the total came out almost unchanged at around 11,000. Better stoves and insulation cancelled out a village four times the size, which is only possible because the efficiency gain was so large. Partial: naming both factors with only one of the two multipliers worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","11,500 divided by 7,000 is about 1.64 cubic metres per person per year. That is another 25 percent cut below the 2.2 already reached, after the easy gains have been taken, which is why efficiency gets harder the further it is pushed. Partial: getting the 1.64 cubic metres with nothing said about it being a further cut on top of the one already made is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Any two: the forest is cut faster than it grows, so standing wood falls year after year and eventually there is not enough to cut; the soil loses the shade and the leaf fall that keep it moist and fed, and it erodes on slopes; the animals and plants that need older trees and fallen wood lose their habitat; and stream temperatures rise and flows become flashier where the cover goes. The chart shows efficiency can hold a total flat for a while, but per person use cannot fall below what a person actually needs, so a village that keeps growing eventually crosses the limit no matter how efficient it becomes. Partial: two effects named with nothing said about per person use not being able to fall below what a person needs is approaching. (MS-ESS3)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Pushes on Ice",short:"Forces",parts:[{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"applying the third law to a push between two objects and relating force and mass to motion",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two skaters stand facing each other on the frozen pond. One pushes once and they slide apart.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Skater</th><th>Mass with gear</th><th>Speed just after the push</th></tr>
     <tr><td>skater carrying the loaded pack</td><td>70 kg</td><td>0.6 m/s</td></tr>
     <tr><td>skater with no pack</td><td>42 kg</td><td>1.0 m/s</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">A sled is then pulled along packed snow with a spring scale.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Pull</th><th>Mass</th><th>Acceleration</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>30 N</td><td>15 kg</td><td>2 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>30 N</td><td>30 kg</td><td>1 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>120 N</td><td>15 kg</td><td>8 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>105 N</td><td>35 kg</td><td>3 m/s each second</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("skate",74)}${ico("sled",74)}${ico("mitten",74)}</div>`,qs:["Only one skater pushed, and both slid. Explain with Newton's third law and compare the sizes of the two forces.","Multiply mass by speed for each skater. Say what you find, then predict the lighter skater's speed if the heavier one had massed 84 kg and still left at 0.6 m/s.","Point to the two trials that change only the mass and the two that change only the pull. Say what each pair proves, then show that trial 4 obeys the rule."],key:["Forces come in pairs. The pushing skater pushed on the other one, and the other one pushed back with a force equal in size and opposite in direction, at the same instant and for the same length of time. The ice is slippery enough that neither is held in place, so both slide away from the other even though only one of them did anything. Partial: saying the other skater pushed back, with nothing said about the two forces being equal in size and opposite in direction, is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Heavier skater: 70 times 0.6 is 42. Lighter skater: 42 times 1.0 is also 42. The two quantities of motion are equal in size and opposite in direction, which is what equal and opposite forces acting for the same time produce. An 84 kg skater at 0.6 m/s carries 50.4, so the 42 kg skater would leave at 50.4 divided by 42, which is 1.2 m/s. Partial: finding both products equal to 42 with the 1.2 m/s prediction not worked out is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Trials 1 and 2 hold the pull at 30 N and double the mass from 15 kg to 30 kg, and the acceleration halves from 2 to 1. Trials 1 and 3 hold the mass at 15 kg and multiply the pull by four, 30 N to 120 N, and the acceleration rises by four, 2 to 8. Trial 4 fits force equals mass times acceleration: 35 kg times 3 m/s each second is 105 N, the pull listed. Partial: naming both pairs of trials with trial 4 not checked against force equals mass times acceleration is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]},{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use all three charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying what changes electric and magnetic forces, relating weight to mass, and naming fields",html:()=>`<p class="big">The latch on the snow gate is an electromagnet. Each trial measures how hard it holds.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Turns of wire</th><th>Current</th><th>Core</th><th>Holding force</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>150</td><td>0.5 A</td><td>iron</td><td>8 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>150</td><td>1.5 A</td><td>iron</td><td>24 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>450</td><td>0.5 A</td><td>iron</td><td>24 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>150</td><td>0.5 A</td><td>none, air</td><td>1 N</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">A plastic comb is charged by drawing it through dry hair and held above paper scraps.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Strokes through the hair</th><th>Scraps lifted from 2 cm</th><th>Scraps lifted from 4 cm</th></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>1</td></tr>
     <tr><td>6</td><td>11</td><td>3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>12</td><td>20</td><td>5</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The same 8 kg of split firewood, weighed in three places.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>Weight on the scale</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the village, sea level</td><td>78.4 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on the moon</td><td>13 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on a world with Earth's mass and twice Earth's radius</td><td>19.6 N</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("key",74)}${ico("compass",74)}${ico("fire",74)}</div>`,qs:["Use the latch chart. Name three things that change the holding force and give the trial pair that shows each.","Use the comb chart. Name the two things that change how many scraps are lifted, and say which has the larger effect over the range shown.","Explain all three firewood weights, then name what occupies the gap between the comb and the paper and between the latch and the gate."],key:["Current: trials 1 and 2, where trebling it from 0.5 A to 1.5 A trebles the hold from 8 N to 24 N. Turns of wire: trials 1 and 3, where trebling them from 150 to 450 also trebles the hold from 8 N to 24 N. The core: trials 1 and 4, where removing the iron drops the hold from 8 N to 1 N. The core change is the largest of the three, cutting the force to an eighth, and it is the only one that costs nothing to run. Partial: all three factors named with trial pairs but no judgment given on which change is the largest is approaching. (MS-PS2)","The charge on the comb, set here by the number of strokes, and the distance to the paper. Charge: at 2 cm the lift goes 3, 11, 20 as the strokes go 2, 6, 12. Distance: at 12 strokes the lift falls from 20 at 2 cm to 5 at 4 cm, a quarter of the value for twice the distance. Over this range distance is the stronger lever, because doubling it alone cuts the force to about a quarter while six times the rubbing raises the lift by about seven times. Partial: naming both factors with no comparison made of which lever is stronger over the range shown is approaching. (MS-PS2)","The firewood holds the same 8 kg of matter everywhere. Weight is the gravitational force on it, which depends on its mass, the mass of the world, and the distance to that world's center. The moon has about an eightieth of Earth's mass, so it pulls with only 13 N. A world with Earth's mass but twice the radius puts the wood twice as far from the center, and the pull falls off with the square of distance, so it reads a quarter of 78.4 N, which is 19.6 N. The gaps are filled by fields: an electric field between comb and paper, a magnetic field between latch and gate, and a gravitational field between the wood and the world. Partial: explaining two of the three weights, or all three with no field named for the gaps, is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Down the Sledding Hill",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["MS-PS3"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"relating kinetic energy to mass and speed and stored energy to the arrangement of objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">A sled is released on the hill above the village and its kinetic energy is measured at the bottom.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Mass with rider</th><th>Speed</th><th>Kinetic energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>40 kg</td><td>3 m/s</td><td>180 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>80 kg</td><td>3 m/s</td><td>360 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>40 kg</td><td>6 m/s</td><td>720 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>40 kg</td><td>1.5 m/s</td><td>45 J</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Stored energy measured for the same 40 kg sled held at the top of three rises, and for the spring on the hut door.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Stored energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>sled held at the top of a 2 m rise</td><td>784 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sled held at the top of a 6 m rise</td><td>2,352 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sled held at the top of a 10 m rise</td><td>3,920 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>door spring stretched 3 cm</td><td>0.45 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>door spring stretched 9 cm</td><td>4.05 J</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sled",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["Compare trials 1 and 2. State how kinetic energy depends on mass, then check trial 4 against trial 1.","Compare trials 1 and 3, then use the rule to predict the energy of the 40 kg sled at 9 m/s.","Use the sled rise rows. State how stored energy depends on the rise, and explain why the number depends on where the measuring starts.","The spring rows go up nine times when the stretch trebles, while the sled rows only treble when the rise trebles. Say what that shows about reading one store's rule onto another."],key:["Doubling the mass from 40 kg to 80 kg at the same 3 m/s doubled the energy from 180 J to 360 J, so at a fixed speed kinetic energy is proportional to mass. Trial 4 halves the speed from 3 m/s to 1.5 m/s and the energy drops to a quarter, 180 J to 45 J, which fits a square relationship with speed rather than a proportional one. Partial: stating the proportion to mass with trial 4 not checked against trial 1 is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Doubling the speed from 3 m/s to 6 m/s multiplied the energy by four, 180 J to 720 J, so kinetic energy goes up with the square of the speed. At 9 m/s the speed is three times trial 1's, so the energy is nine times 180 J, which is 1,620 J. Partial: naming the squared relationship with the 1,620 J prediction not worked out, or a prediction of 540 J that scales the speed once rather than squaring it, is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Trebling the rise from 2 m to 6 m trebled the stored energy from 784 J to 2,352 J, and five times the rise gave five times the energy, 3,920 J at 10 m, so stored energy is proportional to the rise. Where the measuring starts is a choice: measured from the bottom of the hill the sled at the 2 m rise holds 784 J, and measured from the frozen pond further down it would hold more. Stored energy belongs to the arrangement of the sled and Earth and only has a number once a reference position is named. Partial: describing the proportion to the rise with nothing said about the starting point being a choice is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Both stores grow when objects are pushed against the force between them, but the amounts do not follow the same rule. Lifting is proportional to the height, while stretching this spring goes up with the square of the stretch, because each extra centimetre of stretch takes more force than the one before while each extra metre of lift takes the same force as the one before. So a rule found for one store has to be measured again for another, not assumed. Partial: noting that the two rules differ with nothing said about each extra centimetre of stretch taking more force than the last is approaching. (MS-PS3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Across the Frozen Pond",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"relating amplitude to the energy in a wave and using wavelength and frequency to find wave speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">A horn is sounded at the hut and a meter 40 m across the frozen pond records the energy arriving.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Swing of the diaphragm</th><th>Wavelength</th><th>Cycles per second</th><th>Energy at the meter</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>0.1 mm</td><td>1.00 m</td><td>330</td><td>1 unit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>0.3 mm</td><td>1.00 m</td><td>330</td><td>9 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>0.5 mm</td><td>1.00 m</td><td>330</td><td>25 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>0.5 mm</td><td>0.50 m</td><td>660</td><td>25 units</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("megaphone",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}</div>`,qs:["Settings A, B and C differ only in the swing of the diaphragm. Say what the energy column does as that swing grows, and name the relationship.","Find the speed of the sound from row C and again from row D. Say why the two agree and what the frequency had to do when the wavelength halved.","Setting D sounds twice as high as setting C and delivers exactly the same energy. Explain why that is not a contradiction."],key:["Trebling the swing from 0.1 mm to 0.3 mm multiplied the energy by nine, 1 unit to 9 units, and five times the swing gave twenty five times the energy, 25 units at 0.5 mm. The energy goes up with the square of the amplitude, which is why amplitude is the measure of a wave's energy. Partial: saying the energy rises with the swing, with nothing said about it going up with the square of the amplitude, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Speed is wavelength times cycles per second. C: 1.00 m times 330 is 330 m per second. D: 0.50 m times 660 is also 330 m per second. Sound travels at one speed through the same cold air, so halving the wavelength forces the frequency to double. Neither change touched the amplitude, so neither touched the energy. Partial: working out one of the two speeds, or both with nothing said about frequency having to double when the wavelength halves, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Pitch is set by the frequency and energy is set by the amplitude, and they are separate properties of the same wave. D has twice C's frequency, so the ear hears it an octave higher, and the identical 0.5 mm swing means each cycle carries the same energy, so the meter reads the same 25 units. How high a sound is and how much energy it carries are two different questions. Partial: saying pitch and loudness are different, with nothing said about frequency setting one and amplitude the other, is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]},{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Use the chart and the two methods. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading reflection, absorption and transmission from data and comparing a digital with an analog signal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A lamp sends 100 units of light at each material. One meter sits behind it and one beside the lamp.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Material</th><th>Passed through</th><th>Came back</th><th>Neither</th></tr>
     <tr><td>fresh snow, 5 cm</td><td>4</td><td>88</td><td>8</td></tr>
     <tr><td>old grey snow, 5 cm</td><td>3</td><td>61</td><td>36</td></tr>
     <tr><td>clear lake ice, 5 cm</td><td>76</td><td>11</td><td>13</td></tr>
     <tr><td>dark spruce bark</td><td>0</td><td>9</td><td>91</td></tr>
     <tr><td>window glass with frost on it</td><td>54</td><td>33</td><td>13</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Two ways to get the ridge hut's snow depth down to the village, 11 km away.<br>Method 1: a lamp whose brightness stands for the depth in centimetres.<br>Method 2: the same lamp flashed in a pattern standing for three digits.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("snowman",74)}${ico("lantern",74)}</div>`,qs:["Fresh snow sends back 88 and old grey snow 61. Say where the missing 27 went and what that does to how fast each one melts in sunshine.","Clear ice passes 76 and frosted glass passes 54. Explain, using the chart, why you can read print through one and not the other.","A snow squall halves the brightness of both lamps. Say which method still delivers the right depth and why, then name one cost of that method."],key:["It was absorbed. Old grey snow's absorption is 36 against fresh snow's 8, a rise of 28, which covers the 27 point drop in what came back. Absorbed light warms the snow, so the grey snow takes in more than four times as much energy from the same sunshine and melts faster, while fresh white snow sends most of the light straight back and warms slowly. That is why a dirty drift collapses before a clean one. Partial: naming absorption as where the light went with nothing said about the grey snow therefore melting faster is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Clear ice passes most of the light and passes it straight through, so the pattern of light that comes out still matches what went in and print stays readable. Frosted glass passes 54 but its rough surface scatters what passes in all directions, and it also sends 33 back scattered, so the pattern is destroyed even though over half the light gets through. Seeing through something needs light that both passes and keeps its direction. Partial: contrasting the 76 with the 54, with nothing said about transmitted light having to keep its direction, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The flashing pattern. It is a digital signal: each flash only has to be told apart from no flash, so half the brightness still gives a readable pattern and the three digits arrive exactly as sent. The steady brightness is analog, where the value is the brightness, so halving it halves the reported depth and nothing in the signal shows an error. The cost of the digital method is that it takes longer to send, both ends have to agree on the code first, and it cannot report a depth finer than the code allows. Partial: choosing the flashing pattern with nothing said about why the steady lamp hides its own error, or with no cost of the digital method named, is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]}]},{id:26,title:"The Line From the Spring",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Read the situation and the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria and constraints and evaluating competing designs against them",html:()=>`<p class="big">The 400 m water line from the spring to the village freezes most winters, and when it does the village has no water for about two weeks. There is $1,500, four weekends of volunteer time, and no electricity anywhere along the line. The route crosses a stream that has to stay clear for fish.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Cost</th><th>Build time</th><th>Winters it kept water flowing, out of 5</th><th>Needs power on the line</th><th>Blocks the stream</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: bury the pipe 1.5 m deep</td><td>$1,350</td><td>4 weekends</td><td>5</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: wrap the pipe above ground</td><td>$420</td><td>1 weekend</td><td>2</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: electric heat tape along the pipe</td><td>$2,900</td><td>2 weekends</td><td>5</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D: reroute through a culvert under the stream bed</td><td>$900</td><td>3 weekends</td><td>4</td><td>no</td><td>yes, while the work is done</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("drop",74)}${ico("cabin",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}</div>`,qs:["Write out what has to be achieved here, then put each column of the chart under either criteria or constraints.","Name every design that fails a constraint and say which constraint each one fails.","Choose between what is left and defend your choice from the chart. Say what you would test first."],key:["Achieve: keep water moving through 400 m of pipe from the spring to the village all winter. Criterion, the measure of success: the number of winters out of five in which the water kept flowing. Constraints, the limits on any solution: no more than $1,500, no more than four weekends of volunteer labor, no electricity available along the line, and the stream kept clear for fish. Only the winters column is a criterion; cost, time, power and the stream are constraints. Partial: a goal stated with cost or labour filed as criteria rather than constraints is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Design C fails two: $2,900 breaks the $1,500 budget, and it needs power on a line where there is none. Design D fails one: it blocks the stream during the work, and the stream was required to stay clear for fish. Both are out on constraints regardless of how well they perform, and D performs well at 4 of 5. Partial: naming Design C with Design D's blocked stream not identified as a constraint failure is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","A or B, defended from the chart. A is the stronger answer: 5 winters out of 5, $1,350 of the $1,500, and 4 of the 4 weekends, so it meets the criterion fully and fits every limit, with no margin on time. B is the safer bet against anything going wrong, at $420 and one weekend, but 2 winters out of 5 means it fails the actual problem more often than it solves it. The thing to test first for A is whether frost ever reaches 1.5 m in this valley, since that single number decides the whole design, and a soil temperature probe left in over one winter would answer it. Partial: a design chosen and defended from the chart with nothing named that would be tested first is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]},{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing test data to find which change helps and planning the next round of testing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four versions were tested on a 20 m length of the line through one cold spell.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Version</th><th>Wrap thickness</th><th>Depth of pipe</th><th>What happened</th><th>Coldest the water got</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>2 cm</td><td>on the surface</td><td>froze on night 6</td><td>minus 0.4 degrees</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>6 cm</td><td>on the surface</td><td>froze on night 19</td><td>minus 0.2 degrees</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>2 cm</td><td>0.4 m buried</td><td>did not freeze</td><td>plus 1.9 degrees</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>6 cm</td><td>0.4 m buried</td><td>did not freeze</td><td>plus 3.1 degrees</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name the one change between versions 1 and 2, and between 1 and 3, and say what each change did.","Compare the gain from trebling the wrap with the gain from burying the pipe 0.4 m, and say which to spend on.","Write version 5 and predict the result, then name a variable the chart has not tested and say how you would test it."],key:["Versions 1 to 2: only the wrap changed, 2 cm to 6 cm, and the pipe lasted from night 6 to night 19 but still froze, with the water bottoming out at minus 0.2 instead of minus 0.4. Versions 1 to 3: only the depth changed, surface to 0.4 m, and the pipe did not freeze at all, with the water holding at plus 1.9 degrees. Each test moved one variable, which is the only reason either result can be assigned to a cause. Partial: naming both changes with the effect on the freezing night or the low temperature not given is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Trebling the wrap bought 13 extra nights and raised the low point by 0.2 degrees, and the pipe still froze, so it delayed the failure without preventing it. Burying the pipe 0.4 m prevented the failure outright and raised the low point by 2.3 degrees. Depth is what to spend on: insulation only slows heat leaving the pipe, while depth puts the pipe in ground the frost has not reached, which is a different and better answer to the problem. Partial: comparing the two gains with no spending conclusion drawn, or a conclusion drawn with nothing said about the wrap only delaying the failure, is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Any version argued from the chart. The strongest is 2 cm of wrap buried at 0.4 m, since version 3 already succeeded without the extra wrap and version 4's additional 1.2 degrees may not be worth the cost; predict no freezing and a low near plus 2 degrees, and use the margin to decide whether the wrap is needed at all. The untested variable is the length of the cold spell, because all four versions were watched through one spell on 20 m of pipe, so the next test should hold the version 3 settings and run them through a longer and colder winter, or through the whole 400 m where the pipe may be shallower in places. Partial: a version 5 argued from the chart with no untested variable named and no test for it described is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Strong and Thorough",std:["RL.9-10.1"],needs:["RL.8.1"],dir:"Answer out loud. Read the exact words.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Nadia pays close attention to the pond even before anyone asks her to. Prove it with strong and thorough evidence: give at least three details from different parts of the story, and explain what each one shows.</p><div class="passage small-p" style="margin-top:14px">${STORY}</div>`,key:["Three or more details from across the story, each explained, such as: she “walked the edge of the pond before school, the way her grandmother had taught her”; she noticed the ice “was darker” near the inlet; she tapped it and heard “a sound like a spoon on an empty bowl”; she says “Not like this” when Teo brushes her off; she stood on the bank “until her fingers went stiff” before knocking. (HS RL.9-10.1, strong and thorough evidence)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Find the Faulty Leap",std:["RI.9-10.8"],needs:["RI.8.8"],dir:"Answer out loud.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Mr. Prazak writes: “A village that locks its only hill road is telling its older neighbors to stay home.”</p><p class="big">What is wrong with this statement as reasoning? Find what is exaggerated or untrue, and explain the faulty leap.</p>${lookBack()}`,key:["Exaggerated or false: the plan closes the road to cars from December 1 to March 1 only, and Text A says plows and ambulances would still use it, so the village does not “lock” the road. Faulty leap: it jumps from a traffic rule to a claim about what the village is telling people, with no evidence that anyone would stay home. It is an appeal to emotion, not proof. Needs one false or exaggerated statement and the faulty reasoning. (HS RI.9-10.8, false statements and fallacious reasoning)"]},4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Make It Match",std:["L.9-10.1"],needs:["L.8.1"],dir:"Say each sentence the correct way.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Fix this sentence so the parts match: “The plan would cut plowing costs, keeping sledders out of traffic, and to protect the deer.”</li><li>Fix this sentence so the parts match: “Teo likes skating, to shovel the rink, and hot cocoa.”</li></ol>`,key:["The plan would cut plowing costs, keep sledders out of traffic, and protect the deer. All three parts are now plain verbs. (HS L.9-10.1, parallel structure)","Teo likes skating, shoveling the rink, and drinking hot cocoa. Any version with matching forms. (HS L.9-10.1, parallel structure)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Give the Other Side Its Due",std:["W.9-10.1"],needs:["W.8.1"],dir:"Add to your writing in the box on the Write by the Fire stop, or say it out loud.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Add two or three sentences to your argument. State the other side’s strongest point fairly, say what is strong about it, and then explain its limit.</p>`,key:["Develops the counterclaim fairly: states the other side’s best point accurately, names its strength, and explains its limitation with evidence. (HS W.9-10.1, strengths and limitations of claim and counterclaim)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Roots as Exponents",std:["N-RN.2"],needs:["8.EE.A.1"],dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Rewrite 25<sup>1/2</sup> using a root symbol. Then find its value.</li><li>Find the value of 27<sup>1/3</sup>.</li><li>Find the value of 8<sup>2/3</sup>.</li></ol>`,key:["√25 = 5 (HS N-RN.2)","3 (HS N-RN.2)","4, because the cube root of 8 is 2 and 2<sup>2</sup> = 4 (HS N-RN.2)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Two Steps to Eliminate",std:["A-REI.6"],needs:["8.EE.C.8"],dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Solve this system: 2x + 5y = 3 and 3x − 2y = 14.</p>`,key:["x = 4, y = −1. Needs to multiply both equations before eliminating, for example by 2 and by 5. Check: 2(4) + 5(−1) = 3 and 3(4) − 2(−1) = 14. (HS A-REI.6)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Name That Output",std:["F-IF.2"],needs:["8.F.A.1"],dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",html:()=>`<p class="big">g(x) = 5x − 3</p><ol class="qlist"><li>Find g(6).</li><li>Find the value of x that makes g(x) = 42.</li></ol>`,key:["27 (HS F-IF.2)","x = 9 (HS F-IF.2)"]},12:{id:"L12",title:"Level Up: Two Moves in a Row",std:["G-CO.5"],needs:["8.G.A.3"],dir:"Answer out loud.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Triangle DEF moves so that its new corners are D′(2, 1), E′(1, 5), and F′(5, 3). Describe a sequence of transformations that carries triangle DEF onto the new triangle.</p><div class="figure">${sledGrid()}</div>`,key:["A rotation of 90° clockwise about the origin followed by a reflection across the x-axis. That pair is the same as one reflection across the line y = x, which swaps each x and y. (HS G-CO.5)"]}};const DESC={"1.0":"citing the strongest evidence for what a story says and for an inference","1.1":"explaining how lines of dialogue provoke a decision and reveal character","1.2":"figurative language, word choice and tone, and an allusion in a story","2.0":"citing the strongest evidence from two opinion texts","2.1":"tracing and judging an argument, including irrelevant and weak evidence","2.2":"finding where two texts disagree about facts and about interpretation","3.0":"research notes (paraphrase, quote, source credibility, citations and search terms)","4.0":"verbals, active and passive voice, subjunctive mood, and shifts in voice","5.argument":"a 1 to 2 minute spoken argument with evidence from two texts","5.informative":"a 1 to 2 minute spoken explanation of a debate with evidence from two texts","5.narrative":"a 1 to 2 minute spoken narrative","6.argument":"an argument paragraph with evidence from two texts","6.informative":"an informative piece explaining a debate from two texts","6.narrative":"a narrative writing piece","7.0":"sorting rational and irrational numbers and converting between repeating decimals and fractions","7.1":"square root and cube root equations and evaluating roots","8.0":"using the properties of integer exponents","9.0":"slope as unit rate and comparing proportional relationships from a graph and an equation","10.0":"systems of two linear equations (graph, algebra, number of solutions, word problem)","11.0":"identifying functions from tables, graphs, and a rule","11.1":"telling linear from nonlinear functions using y = mx + b","12.0":"coordinates after a translation, reflection, rotation, and dilation","13.0":"Pythagorean Theorem problems in two and three dimensions","14.0":"interpreting and constructing a scatter plot"};STOPS.forEach(st=>st.parts.forEach((p,i)=>{p.desc=DESC[st.id+"."+(p.wt||i)]||p.desc||""}));Object.values(LEVELUPS).forEach(l=>{l.n=l.key.length});const LOG_NAMES={readq:"Questions read aloud",passage:"Text read aloud by an adult",repeat:"Directions repeated or reworded",vocab:"Word or phrase explained by teacher",hint:"Hint or first step given",fewer:"Fewer questions",time:"Extra time",scribe:"Adult typed or wrote the student’s words",brk:"Break"};const READ_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","RL.9-10.1","RI.9-10.8"];const WRITE_STD=["W.8.1","W.8.2","W.8.3","W.9-10.1"];const MATH_STD=["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1","N-RN.2","A-REI.6","F-IF.2","G-CO.5"];const TEXT_STD=["RL.8.1","RI.8.1","W.8.8","RL.9-10.1","RI.9-10.8"];function stopLogs(st){const out=["readq"];if(READ_STD.some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("passage");out.push("repeat","vocab","hint","fewer","time");if(WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("scribe");out.push("brk");return out}const dl=a=>`<dl>${a.map(x=>`<dt>${x[0]}</dt><dd>${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function stopSpeak(){try{window.speechSynthesis&&window.speechSynthesis.cancel()}catch(e){}}function guardSpeech(){["mapBtn","teacherBtn","newBtn","prevBtn","gemBtn","skipBtn"].forEach(i=>{const el=document.getElementById(i);if(el&&!el.dataset.tts){el.dataset.tts="1";el.addEventListener("click",stopSpeak)}})}function speakText(t){stopSpeak();guardSpeech();try{const u=new SpeechSynthesisUtterance(t);u.rate=+(document.getElementById("ttsRate")||{value:.9}).value;window.speechSynthesis.speak(u)}catch(e){}}function textOf(sel){return[...document.querySelectorAll(sel)].map(e=>e.textContent.trim()).join(" ")}function listenHtml(st){const two=isTexts(st);return`<div class="blk-row">${two?`<button class="chip" data-say="a">Play Text A</button><button class="chip" data-say="b">Play Text B</button>`:`<button class="chip" data-say="p">Play the story</button>`}<button class="chip" data-say="q">Play the questions</button><button class="chip" data-say="x">Stop</button></div><label class="small">Speed <select id="ttsRate"><option value="0.75">Slow</option><option value="0.9" selected>Normal</option><option value="1.1">Fast</option></select></label><p class="small">Uses this computer’s voice. If you hear nothing, check the volume, then read the text aloud yourself and mark “Text read aloud by an adult.”</p>`}function listenBind(b){b.querySelectorAll("[data-say]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.say,card="#stopView .card ";if(k==="x")return stopSpeak();const d=document.querySelector(card+"details.look-back");if(d)d.open=true;if(k==="p")speakText(textOf(card+".passage"));if(k==="a")speakText(textOf(card+".texts > div:nth-child(1)"));if(k==="b")speakText(textOf(card+".texts > div:nth-child(2)"));if(k==="q")speakText(textOf(card+"ol.qlist > li:not([hidden]), "+card+"> .big"))})}const GLOSS={1:[["inlet","the place where a creek flows into a pond"],["relay","a race where teammates take turns, one after another"],["anchor a team","skate the last leg of a relay for your team"],["warming hut","a small heated building where people go to get warm"],["cones","small markers set on the ice to show the lanes"],["drift","snow that wind has piled up in one spot"]],2:[["nurse practitioner","a nurse with extra training who can examine and treat patients"],["gate","a bar or arm across a road to keep cars out"],["salted","spread with salt so ice on the road melts"],["estimate","a careful guess made with numbers"],["packed ice","snow pressed down until it turns to hard, slick ice"],["public works","the village office in charge of roads, plowing, and water"],["merchants","shop owners"]]};function glossHtml(st){return dl(GLOSS[isTexts(st)?2:1])}const TERMS=[["cite","point to the exact words in the text that prove your answer"],["inference","something the text does not say outright, but that you can figure out from clues"],["claim","what the writer wants you to believe or do"],["reason","why the writer says the claim is true"],["evidence","facts, numbers, examples, or quotes that back up a reason"],["relevant","actually about the claim"],["sufficient","enough to prove the point"],["sound reasoning","thinking that makes sense and follows from the evidence"],["tone","the feeling or attitude the words create"],["figurative language","words that mean something beyond their plain meaning, like a comparison"],["allusion","a quick reference to a well-known story, person, or event"],["fact or interpretation","a fact can be checked; an interpretation is what someone thinks the facts mean"]];const FRAMES=["I think ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The strongest evidence is “____” because ____.","This line shows that ____ (character) is ____ because ____.","The author compares ____ to ____. This makes the reader feel ____.","Text A says ____, but Text B says ____.","This evidence is weak because ____.","This detail does not matter to the claim because ____."];function verbsHtml(){return`<h4>Verbals: verb forms doing another job</h4>`+dl([["Gerund","ends in -ing and acts as a noun. <em>Skating is hard work.</em>"],["Participle","ends in -ing or -ed and describes a noun. <em>The drifting snow stopped us.</em>"],["Infinitive","to + verb. <em>She hoped to finish.</em>"]])+`<h4>Voice</h4>`+dl([["Active","the subject does the action. <em>The crew plowed the road.</em>"],["Passive","the subject receives the action. <em>The road was plowed by the crew.</em>"]])+`<h4>Mood</h4>`+dl([["Indicative","states a fact. <em>It is snowing.</em>"],["Imperative","gives a command. <em>Shut the door.</em>"],["Interrogative","asks a question. <em>Is it snowing?</em>"],["Conditional","tells what would or could happen. <em>I would go if I could.</em>"],["Subjunctive","a wish or something not true. Uses were. <em>If she were taller, she could reach.</em>"]])+`<p class="small">Keep the same voice and mood through a sentence.</p>`}const PLAN={argument:["My claim","Reason 1 and evidence (which text?)","Reason 2 and evidence (which text?)","What the other side says, and my answer","Conclusion"],informative:["The topic and what I will cover","What Side A wants and its best evidence","What Side B wants and its best evidence","Where they disagree","Conclusion"],narrative:["Who, where, and when","The plan and what changed it","What happened first, next, last","A line of dialogue I will use","How it ended and what it meant"]};function planHtml(st){const S=E.state;S.plan=S.plan||{};const wt=curWT(st),v=S.plan[wt]||[];return`<div class="planner">${PLAN[wt].map((x,i)=>`<label>${x}<textarea data-pi="${i}">${(v[i]||"").replace(/</g,"&lt;")}</textarea></label>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Your plan is saved for the speaking stop and the writing stop.</p>`}function planBind(b,r,st){b.querySelectorAll("[data-pi]").forEach(t=>t.oninput=()=>{const S=E.state,wt=curWT(st);S.plan=S.plan||{};S.plan[wt]=S.plan[wt]||[];S.plan[wt][+t.dataset.pi]=t.value;E.save()})}const SENT={argument:{s:["The village should ____ because ____.","According to Text ____, ____.","Some people argue that ____. However, ____.","This evidence shows that ____.","For these reasons, ____."],w:["because","for example","however","although","in addition","as a result","therefore","on the other hand"]},informative:{s:["The debate in Birchfold is about ____.","One side believes ____.","According to Text ____, ____.","In contrast, the other side ____.","Overall, ____."],w:["for example","in contrast","similarly","also","as a result","in addition","overall"]},narrative:{s:["It was ____ when ____.","At first, ____.","“____,” ____ said.","Then the weather ____.","Looking back, I realized ____."],w:["at first","meanwhile","by morning","suddenly","after that","by the time","finally","looking back"]}};function sentHtml(st){const d=SENT[curWT(st)];return`<ul class="starters">${d.s.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul><h4>Linking words</h4><div class="wordbank">${d.w.map(x=>`<span>${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`}const CHECK={argument:["I stated my claim.","I named the other side and showed how my claim is different.","I gave reasons with evidence and named the text.","I used linking words.","I kept a formal style (no slang).","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],informative:["I have a heading.","I introduced the topic.","I organized it by side or by idea.","I used facts or a quote from each text.","I used linking words and topic words.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],narrative:["I set the scene.","Events are in an order that makes sense.","I used dialogue.","I used details the reader can see, hear, or feel.","I used time words.","My ending reflects on what happened.","I reread it for mistakes."]};function checkHtml(st){return`<ul class="card-list" style="list-style:none;padding-left:0">${CHECK[curWT(st)].map(x=>`<li><label style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:flex-start"><input type="checkbox" style="width:20px;height:20px;margin-top:4px"> ${x}</label></li>`).join("")}</ul>`}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){let e=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-").replace(/\^/g,"**").replace(/√\(/g,"Math.sqrt(").replace(/∛\(/g,"Math.cbrt(");if(!/^[0-9+\-*/().\sMathsqrcb]*$/.test(e)||!e)return"";try{const o=(e.match(/\(/g)||[]).length-(e.match(/\)/g)||[]).length;if(o>0)e+=")".repeat(o);const v=Function('"use strict";return ('+e+")")();return Number.isFinite(v)?String(+v.toPrecision(10)):"Error"}catch(x){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","^","+","C","√(","∛(","x²","x³","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k==="^"?"xʸ":k==="√("?"√":k==="∛("?"∛":k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">For a power, press xʸ between the base and the exponent. Use ( ) around a negative exponent.</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.replace(/(√\(|∛\(|.)$/,"");calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷^x]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k==="x²"?"^2":k==="x³"?"^3":k}r()})}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function sqHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>n</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=15;n++)h+=`<th>${n}</th>`;h+=`</tr><tr><th>n²</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=15;n++)h+=`<td>${n*n}</td>`;h+=`</tr><tr><th>n³</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=15;n++)h+=`<td>${n<=6?n*n*n:""}</td>`;return h+`</tr></table></div><p class="small">n² means n × n. n³ means n × n × n. To find a square root, find the number in the n² row and look up to n.</p>`}function expHtml(){return`<table class="meas">`+[["Multiply, same base: add the exponents","a<sup>m</sup> × a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m+n</sup>"],["Divide, same base: subtract the exponents","a<sup>m</sup> ÷ a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m−n</sup>"],["Power of a power: multiply the exponents","(a<sup>m</sup>)<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>mn</sup>"],["Zero exponent","a<sup>0</sup> = 1"],["Negative exponent: flip it to a fraction","a<sup>−n</sup> = 1/a<sup>n</sup>"]].map(r=>`<tr><th>${r[0]}</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">${r[1]}</td></tr>`).join("")+`</table>`}const FORM={"8.NS.A.1":["Rational and irrational",ul(["<strong>Rational:</strong> can be written as a fraction of two integers. Its decimal ends or repeats.","<strong>Irrational:</strong> cannot be written as a fraction. Its decimal never ends and never repeats."])],"8.EE.A.2":["Roots",ul(["If x<sup>2</sup> = p, then x = √p or x = −√p.","If x<sup>3</sup> = p, then x = ∛p.","√ means “what number times itself gives this?”"])],"8.EE.B.5":["Slope and unit rate",`<p class="formula">slope = rise ÷ run = change in y ÷ change in x</p>`+ul(["In y = kx, the number k is the unit rate and the slope.","A proportional relationship is a straight line through (0, 0)."])],"8.EE.C.8":["Systems of equations",ul(["The solution is the point (x, y) that makes both equations true. On a graph it is where the lines cross.","Same slope, different y-intercepts: no solution.","Same line: infinitely many solutions.","Substitution: if both equations say y = ..., set the two right sides equal and solve for x."])],"8.F.A.1":["Functions",ul(["A function is a rule that gives each input exactly one output.","Two inputs can share an output. One input cannot have two outputs.","Vertical line test: a vertical line touches the graph of a function only once."])],"8.F.A.3":["Linear functions",`<p class="formula">y = mx + b</p>`+ul(["m is the slope. b is the y-intercept.","The graph is a straight line. The change is the same every step."])],"8.G.B.7":["Pythagorean Theorem",`<svg viewBox="0 0 220 130" width="220" role="img" aria-label="Right triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c" style="display:block;margin:0 auto"><polygon points="20,110 190,110 20,15" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="20" y="96" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="105" y="128" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">b</text><text x="8" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">a</text><text x="116" y="54" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">c</text></svg><p class="formula">a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup></p>`+ul(["a and b are the legs. c is the hypotenuse, the longest side, across from the right angle.","For a box: length<sup>2</sup> + width<sup>2</sup> + height<sup>2</sup> = diagonal<sup>2</sup>."])],"8.SP.A.1":["Scatter plot words",ul(["<strong>Positive association:</strong> as x goes up, y goes up.","<strong>Negative association:</strong> as x goes up, y goes down.","<strong>Linear:</strong> the points follow a straight line.","<strong>Nonlinear:</strong> the points follow a curve.","<strong>Outlier:</strong> a point far from the rest.","<strong>Cluster:</strong> a group of points close together."])]};function formHtml(st){return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`).join("")}function transHtml(){return`<table class="meas">`+[["Translate right a, up b","(x, y) → (x + a, y + b)"],["Reflect across the x-axis","(x, y) → (x, −y)"],["Reflect across the y-axis","(x, y) → (−x, y)"],["Reflect across the line y = x","(x, y) → (y, x)"],["Rotate 90° counterclockwise about the origin","(x, y) → (−y, x)"],["Rotate 90° clockwise about the origin","(x, y) → (y, −x)"],["Rotate 180° about the origin","(x, y) → (−x, −y)"],["Dilate by scale factor k from the origin","(x, y) → (kx, ky)"]].map(r=>`<tr><th>${r[0]}</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">${r[1]}</td></tr>`).join("")+`</table><p class="small">Left and down are negative moves.</p>`}const EX={"8.NS.A.1":["Turn 0.222... into a fraction",["Let x = 0.222...","Multiply by 10: 10x = 2.222...","Subtract: 10x − x = 2.222... − 0.222..., so 9x = 2","Divide: x = 2/9"]],"8.EE.B.5":["Find the slope from a graph",["Pick two points on the line, such as (0, 0) and (5, 15).","Rise: 15 − 0 = 15. Run: 5 − 0 = 5.","Slope = 15 ÷ 5 = 3. The unit rate is 3 for every 1."]],"8.EE.C.8":["Solve y = 4x + 1 and y = x + 10",["Both equal y, so set them equal: 4x + 1 = x + 10","Subtract x: 3x + 1 = 10","Subtract 1: 3x = 9, so x = 3","Put x back in: y = 3 + 10 = 13","Solution: (3, 13). Check it in the other equation: 4(3) + 1 = 13."]],"8.G.B.7":["Find the missing side",["Legs are 7 and 24. Find the hypotenuse c.","7<sup>2</sup> + 24<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>","49 + 576 = 625","c = √625 = 25","To find a leg instead, subtract: c<sup>2</sup> − a<sup>2</sup> = b<sup>2</sup>."]]};function exHtml(st){return st.std.filter(x=>EX[x]).map(x=>`<h4>${EX[x][0]}</h4><ol class="card-list">${EX[x][1].map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ol>`).join("")+`<p class="small">This is a different problem from the ones on the stop. Use the same steps.</p>`}let gridPts=[],gridJoin=false;function pgridHtml(){return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-g="join" aria-pressed="${gridJoin}">Connect the points</button><button class="chip" data-g="undo">Undo</button><button class="chip" data-g="clear">Clear</button></div>`+gridFig({alt:"Blank coordinate grid from negative 10 to 10. Click to plot points.",cls:"pgrid",x0:-10,x1:10,y0:-10,y1:10,sx:20,sy:20,fs:9,lx:2,ly:2,pr:5,pts:gridPts,paths:gridJoin&&gridPts.length>1?[{pts:gridPts,c:"var(--coral)"}]:[]})+`<p class="small">Click the grid to plot a point. Points: ${gridPts.map(p=>`(${p[0]}, ${p[1]})`).join(", ")||"none yet"}</p>`}function pgridBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-g]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=x.dataset.g;if(g==="join")gridJoin=!gridJoin;if(g==="undo")gridPts.pop();if(g==="clear")gridPts=[];r()});const svg=b.querySelector("svg.pgrid");svg.onclick=e=>{const q=svg.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*454/q.width,vy=(e.clientY-q.top)*448/q.height,x=Math.round((vx-34)/20)-10,y=10-Math.round((vy-18)/20);if(Math.abs(x)>10||Math.abs(y)>10)return;gridPts.push([x,y]);r()}}const TOOLS={listen:{name:"Computer reads it aloud",std:TEXT_STD,ok:()=>"speechSynthesis"in window,html:listenHtml,bind:listenBind},gloss:{name:"Word help",std:TEXT_STD,html:glossHtml},terms:{name:"Reading terms",std:READ_STD,html:()=>dl(TERMS)},frames:{name:"Answer frames",std:READ_STD,html:()=>`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${FRAMES.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`},verbs:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.8.1"],html:verbsHtml},plan:{name:"Planner",std:["SL.8.4"].concat(WRITE_STD),html:planHtml,bind:planBind},sent:{name:"Sentence starters",std:["SL.8.4"].concat(WRITE_STD),html:sentHtml},check:{name:"Writing checklist",std:WRITE_STD,html:checkHtml},calc:{name:"Calculator",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},sq:{name:"Squares and cubes chart",std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.G.B.7","N-RN.2"],html:sqHtml},exp:{name:"Exponent rules",std:["8.EE.A.1","N-RN.2"],html:expHtml},form:{name:"Formula and definition card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},trans:{name:"Transformation rules",std:["8.G.A.3","G-CO.5"],html:transHtml},ex:{name:"Worked example",std:Object.keys(EX),html:exHtml},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid",std:["8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3","8.SP.A.1","G-CO.5","A-REI.6"],html:pgridHtml,bind:pgridBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"listen",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the computer read the text aloud, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"terms",std:["RI.8.8","RL.8.4"],msg:"The reading terms card defines words like relevant, sufficient, and allusion. This stop then shows applying those ideas with a reference."},{t:"frames",msg:"Answer frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and thinking the student supplied."},{t:"verbs",std:["L.8.1"],msg:"The grammar card defines verbals, voice, and mood with examples, so this stop shows using a reference, not recall."},{t:"plan",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["SL.8.4"]),msg:"The planner supplies the organization, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["SL.8.4"]),msg:"Sentence starters supply the linking words and the conclusion opener, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"check",msg:"The checklist names what to include. Note that the student used it to revise."},{t:"calc",std:["8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","N-RN.2"],msg:"The calculator can evaluate powers and roots. Rate on whether the student can show the rule or the steps, not only the value."},{t:"calc",std:["8.NS.A.1"],msg:"The calculator shows the decimal for 5/6, so count that question as supported."},{t:"sq",std:["8.EE.A.2"],msg:"The chart gives square and cube roots, so count the “evaluate” question as supported."},{t:"exp",std:["8.EE.A.1"],msg:"With the exponent rules card, this stop shows using the rules, not remembering them."},{t:"form",msg:"The card gives the formulas and definitions for this stop, so it shows applying them with a reference, not recall."},{t:"trans",std:["8.G.A.3"],msg:"The rules card gives the coordinate rule for each move, so this stop shows applying given rules, not describing the effect independently."},{t:"trans",std:["G-CO.5"],msg:"The rules card lists the reflection across y = x, so the Level Up then shows matching a given rule to the picture."},{t:"ex",msg:"A worked example models the method. Count the matching question as supported."},{t:"scribe",msg:"If an adult typed or wrote, rate the ideas and organization. Do not rate conventions the adult supplied."},{t:"fewer",msg:"If questions were skipped, change the “of” number in the score to the number the student tried."},{t:"hint",msg:"Note which question got the hint, and count that question as supported."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,logNames:LOG_NAMES,stopLogs,onToolChange:()=>stopSpeak()}}});
